window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-25"]=[{"id":"q3-154","q":"3:154","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:154 · Security after grief, destiny and the rest of the realized","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 422–424","cairo":"I:422–424","source_page_first":422,"source_page_last":424,"ar":"ثُمَّ أَنْزَلَ عَلَيْكُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ الْغَمِّ أَمَنَةً نُعاساً يَغْشى طائِفَةً مِنْكُمْ وَطائِفَةٌ قَدْ أَهَمَّتْهُمْ أَنْفُسُهُمْ يَظُنُّونَ بِاللَّهِ غَيْرَ الْحَقِّ ظَنَّ الْجاهِلِيَّةِ يَقُولُونَ هَلْ لَنا مِنَ الْأَمْرِ مِنْ شَيْءٍ قُلْ إِنَّ الْأَمْرَ كُلَّهُ لِلَّهِ يُخْفُونَ فِي أَنْفُسِهِمْ ما لا يُبْدُونَ لَكَ يَقُولُونَ لَوْ كانَ لَنا مِنَ الْأَمْرِ شَيْءٌ ما قُتِلْنا هاهُنا قُلْ لَوْ كُنْتُمْ فِي بُيُوتِكُمْ لَبَرَزَ الَّذِينَ كُتِبَ عَلَيْهِمُ الْقَتْلُ إِلى مَضاجِعِهِمْ وَلِيَبْتَلِيَ اللَّهُ ما فِي صُدُورِكُمْ وَلِيُمَحِّصَ ما فِي قُلُوبِكُمْ وَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ بِذاتِ الصُّدُورِ (154)\nقلت: (نعاسا) : بدل من (أمنة) ، أو هو المفعول، و (أمنة) : حال منه، مقدمة، أو مفعول له، أي: أنزل عليكم نعاساً لأجل الأمنة، أو حال من كاف (عليكم) ، أي: أنزل عليكم حال كونكم آمنين. والأمنة: مصدر أمِن، كالعظَمة والغَلَبة.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ثُمَّ أَنْزَلَ عَلَيْكُمْ أيها المؤمنون مِنْ بَعْدِ الْغَمِّ الذي أصابكم بموت إخوانكم، والإرجاف بقتل نبيكم، الأمن والطمأنينة، حتى أخذكم النعاس وأنتم في الحرب. قال أبو طلحة: (غَشينَا النعاسُ ونحن في المصافّ، حتى كان السيف يسقط من يد أحدنا فيأخذه، ثم يسقط فيأخذه) . وقال الزبير رضي الله عنه: لقد رأيتني حين اشتدّ الخوف، ونحن مع النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم، أرسل الله- تعالى- علينا النوم، والله إني لأسمع قول معتب، والنعاس يغشاني، ما أسمعه إلا كالحلُم: (لو كان لنا من الأمر شيء ما قتلنا هاهنا) .\nثم إن هذا النعاس إنما يَغْشى طائِفَةً مِنْكُمْ وهم المؤمنون، أو: هذه الأمنة إنما تغشى طائفة منكم، وأما المنافقون فقد أَهَمَّتْهُمْ أَنْفُسُهُمْ، أي: أوقعتهم في الهموم والغموم، أو ما يهمهم إلا أنفسهم، يُدبرون خلاصها\nونجاتها، فقد طارت قلوبهم من الخوف، فلا يتصور في حقهم النوم، يَظُنُّونَ بِاللَّهِ غَيْرَ الْحَقِّ أي: غير الظن الحق، لأنهم ظنّوا أنه لا ينصر- عليه الصلاة والسلام، وأن أمره مضمحل، أو ظنوا أنه قتل، ظنّاً كظن الجاهلية، أهل الشرك، يَقُولُونَ أي: بعضهم لبعض: هَلْ لَنا مِنَ الْأَمْرِ مِنْ شَيْءٍ أي: عُزلنا عن تدبر أنفسنا، فلم يبق لنا من الأمر من شيء. قاله ابنُ أُبي، لَما بلغه قتل الخزرج.\nقُلْ لهم يا محمد: إِنَّ الْأَمْرَ كُلَّهُ لِلَّهِ ليس بيد غيره شيء من التدبير والاختيار، حال كون المنافقين يُخْفُونَ فِي أَنْفُسِهِمْ من الكفر والنفاق ما لا يُبْدُونَ لَكَ يَقُولُونَ لَوْ كانَ لَنا مِنَ الْأَمْرِ شَيْءٌ ما قُتِلْنا هاهُنا أي: لو كان تدبيراً أو اختياراً ما خرجنا مع محمد حتى نقتل هاهنا ويقتل رؤساؤنا. قُلْ لهم يا محمد:\nأخرجتكم القدرة فى سلسلة المقادير، رغماً على أنفكم، فلو كُنْتُمْ فِي بُيُوتِكُمْ آمنين لَبَرَزَ الَّذِينَ كُتِبَ عَلَيْهِمُ الْقَتْلُ، ووصل أجلهم إِلى مَضاجِعِهِمْ ومصارعهم، رغماً على أنفهم، فإن الله قدَّر الأمور ودبرها في سابق أزله، لا معقب لحكمه، وإنما فعل ذلك، وأخرجكم إلى المعركة لِيَبْتَلِيَ اللَّهُ ما فِي صُدُورِكُمْ أي: يختبر ما فيها من الخير أو الشر، وَلِيُمَحِّصَ ما فِي قُلُوبِكُمْ أي: يكشف ما فيها من النفاق أو الإخلاص، فقد ظهر خبث سريرتكم ومرض قلوبكم بالنفاق الذي تمكن فيه، وَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ بِذاتِ الصُّدُورِ أي: بخفاياها قبل إظهارها. وفيه وعد ووعيد وتنبيه على أنه غني عن الابتلاء، وإنما فعل ذلك ليُميِّز المؤمنين ويُظهَر حال المنافقين. قاله البيضاوي.\nالإشارة: ثم أنزل عليكم أيها الواصلون المتمكنون، أو من تعلق بكم من السائرين، من بعد غم المجاهدة وتعب المراقبة أمنة في قلوبكم بالطمأنينة بشهود الله، وراحة في جوارحكم من تعب الخدمة في السير إلى الله، حتى وصلتم فنمتم في ظل الأمن والأمان، وسكنتم في جوار الكريم المنان.\nقال بعض العارفين: (إذا انتقلت المعاملة إلى القلوب استراحت الجوارح) ، (١) وهذه الراحة إنما تحصل للعارفين، أو من تعلق بهم من المريدين، وطائفة من غيرهم وهم المتفقرة الجاهلون، الذين لا شيخ لهم، قد أهمتهم أنفسهم، تارة تصرعهم وتارة يصرعونها، تارة تُشرق عليهم أنوارُ التوجه، فيقوى رجاؤهم في الفتح، وتارة تنقبض عنهم فيظنون بالله غير الحق، ظن الجاهلية، يقولون: هل لنا من الفتح من شيء؟.\nقل لهم: (إن الأمر كله لله) يوصل من يشاء ويبعد من يشاء، يخفون في أنفسهم من العيوب والخواطر الرديئة ما لا يبدون لك، فإذا طال عليهم الفتح، وغلب عليهم الفقر، ندموا على ما فاتهم من التمتع بالدنيا، يقولون: لو كان لنا من الأمر شيء ما قتلنا هاهنا بالذل والفقر والجوع، قل لهم: ذلك الذي سبق في علم الله، لا محيد لأحد عنه، ليظهر الصادق في الطلب من الكاذب، [كن صادقاً تجد مرشداً] ، فلو صدقتم في الطلب لأرشدكم إلى من يُوصلكم ويريحكم من التعب. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nثم ذكر الحق تعالى علة انهزام من انهزم، فقال:","quran_ar":"ثُمَّ أَنْزَلَ عَلَيْكُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِ الْغَمِّ أَمَنَةً نُعاساً يَغْشى طائِفَةً مِنْكُمْ وَطائِفَةٌ قَدْ أَهَمَّتْهُمْ أَنْفُسُهُمْ يَظُنُّونَ بِاللَّهِ غَيْرَ الْحَقِّ ظَنَّ الْجاهِلِيَّةِ يَقُولُونَ هَلْ لَنا مِنَ الْأَمْرِ مِنْ شَيْءٍ قُلْ إِنَّ الْأَمْرَ كُلَّهُ لِلَّهِ يُخْفُونَ فِي أَنْفُسِهِمْ ما لا يُبْدُونَ لَكَ يَقُولُونَ لَوْ كانَ لَنا مِنَ الْأَمْرِ شَيْءٌ ما قُتِلْنا هاهُنا قُلْ لَوْ كُنْتُمْ فِي بُيُوتِكُمْ لَبَرَزَ الَّذِينَ كُتِبَ عَلَيْهِمُ الْقَتْلُ إِلى مَضاجِعِهِمْ وَلِيَبْتَلِيَ اللَّهُ ما فِي صُدُورِكُمْ وَلِيُمَحِّصَ ما فِي قُلُوبِكُمْ وَاللَّهُ عَلِيمٌ بِذاتِ الصُّدُورِ (154)","quran_en":"154. Then, after the grief, He sent down upon you security, a drowsiness enveloping a group among you; while another group was preoccupied with itself, thinking of God what was not true—the thinking of the Age of Ignorance. They said, “Do we have any part in the affair?” Say, “The whole affair belongs to God.” They conceal within themselves what they do not disclose to you. They say, “If we had any say in the affair, we would not have been killed here.” Say, “Even if you had been in your houses, those for whom killing was decreed would have gone forth to their resting places.” This was so God might test what is in your breasts and purify what is in your hearts; God knows what hearts contain.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Nuʿās (نعاسًا) and amana (أمنة)","html":"<p>I say: “Drowsiness” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">نُعاسًا<\/bdi>) may stand as a substitute (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بدل<\/bdi>) for “security” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أمنة<\/bdi>), or it may be the object (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعول<\/bdi>), with <strong>amana<\/strong> an anticipatory circumstantial accusative (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حال مقدمة<\/bdi>), or an accusative of purpose (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مفعول له<\/bdi>): He sent down drowsiness on account of security. It can also be a circumstantial expression linked to the pronoun <strong>-kum<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كم<\/bdi>) in “upon you”: He sent it while you were secure. <strong>Amana<\/strong> is a verbal noun (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مصدر<\/bdi>) from <strong>amina<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أمِن<\/bdi>), like <strong>ʿaẓama<\/strong> and <strong>ghalaba<\/strong>.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “Drowsiness” ( نُعاسًا ) may stand as a substitute ( بدل ) for “security” ( أمنة ), or it may be the object ( مفعول ), with amana an anticipatory circumstantial accusative ( حال مقدمة ), or an accusative of purpose ( مفعول له ): He sent down drowsiness on account of security. It can also be a circumstantial expression linked to the pronoun -kum ( كم ) in “upon you”: He sent it while you were secure. Amana is a verbal noun ( مصدر ) from amina ( أمِن ), like ʿaẓama and ghalaba ."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Drowsiness among the believers, anxiety among the hypocrites","html":"<p>After the grief of losing your brothers and hearing rumours of your Prophet's death, God sent you security and tranquillity, to the point that drowsiness overcame you while still drawn up in battle. Abū Ṭalḥa said that sleepiness overtook them in the ranks so that a sword would fall from a man's hand, he would retrieve it, and it would fall again. Al-Zubayr said that amid the danger God sent them sleep and he heard Muʿattib saying, “If we had any part in the matter, we would not have been killed here,” as though he heard it only in a dream.<\/p><p>This drowsiness—or this security—overtook only one group: the believers. Another group, the hypocrites, were plunged into worry and sorrow by their own souls; or it means that they cared only for themselves, planning how to escape and survive; their hearts fluttered with fear, and sleep was impossible for them. They entertained false thoughts about God, imagining He would not help the Prophet, that his cause would perish, or that he had already been killed: the presumptions of the pagan Age of Ignorance (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ظن الجاهلية<\/bdi>). They asked one another whether they had any share in directing events, as though they had been deprived of all control over their own destiny. Ibn ʿAjība attributes this to Ibn Ubayy after he heard of Khazraj casualties.<\/p><p>“Say: the affair in its entirety belongs to God.” No management (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تدبير<\/bdi>) or choice (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">اختيار<\/bdi>) lies in anyone else's hands. Meanwhile they concealed their unbelief and hypocrisy, saying privately that, had the choice rested with them, they would not have marched with Muḥammad only to be killed and lose their chiefs. Say to them: divine power brought you out under the chain of decreed events (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سلسلة المقادير<\/bdi>), despite yourselves. Even if you had stayed secure in your houses, those appointed to die would have gone forth to their places of death (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مضاجع<\/bdi> / <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مصارع<\/bdi>). God arranged events in His pre-eternal knowledge, and His judgment admits no reversal. He brought you to battle to make manifest what is in your breasts—good or evil—and to disclose what is in your hearts—hypocrisy or sincerity. Thus the corruption of their inner disposition and the malady of hypocrisy became visible. God knew what lay hidden before its disclosure; the verse contains promise, threat and the reminder that He has no need to learn through trial. Rather, He distinguishes believers from hypocrites and makes their conditions publicly apparent. Ibn ʿAjība cites al-Bayḍāwī.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"After the grief of losing your brothers and hearing rumours of your Prophet's death, God sent you security and tranquillity, to the point that drowsiness overcame you while still drawn up in battle. Abū Ṭalḥa said that sleepiness overtook them in the ranks so that a sword would fall from a man's hand, he would retrieve it, and it would fall again. Al-Zubayr said that amid the danger God sent them sleep and he heard Muʿattib saying, “If we had any part in the matter, we would not have been killed here,” as though he heard it only in a dream. This drowsiness—or this security—overtook only one group: the believers. Another group, the hypocrites, were plunged into worry and sorrow by their own souls; or it means that they cared only for themselves, planning how to escape and survive; their hearts fluttered with fear, and sleep was impossible for them. They entertained false thoughts about God, imagining He would not help the Prophet, that his cause would perish, or that he had already been killed: the presumptions of the pagan Age of Ignorance ( ظن الجاهلية ). They asked one another whether they had any share in directing events, as though they had been deprived of all control over their own destiny. Ibn ʿAjība attributes this to Ibn Ubayy after he heard of Khazraj casualties. “Say: the affair in its entirety belongs to God.” No management ( تدبير ) or choice ( اختيار ) lies in anyone else's hands. Meanwhile they concealed their unbelief and hypocrisy, saying privately that, had the choice rested with them, they would not have marched with Muḥammad only to be killed and lose their chiefs. Say to them: divine power brought you out under the chain of decreed events ( سلسلة المقادير ), despite yourselves. Even if you had stayed secure in your houses, those appointed to die would have gone forth to their places of death ( مضاجع / مصارع ). God arranged events in His pre-eternal knowledge, and His judgment admits no reversal. He brought you to battle to make manifest what is in your breasts—good or evil—and to disclose what is in your hearts—hypocrisy or sincerity. Thus the corruption of their inner disposition and the malady of hypocrisy became visible. God knew what lay hidden before its disclosure; the verse contains promise, threat and the reminder that He has no need to learn through trial. Rather, He distinguishes believers from hypocrites and makes their conditions publicly apparent. Ibn ʿAjība cites al-Bayḍāwī."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The composure of those who arrive and the worry of seekers without guidance","html":"<p>After the grief of spiritual struggle (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المجاهدة<\/bdi>) and the labour of vigilance (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المراقبة<\/bdi>), God sends security into the hearts of those who have arrived and become firmly established (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الواصلون المتمكنون<\/bdi>), and those wayfarers attached to them: peace in witnessing God, and rest in the bodily faculties after the strain of serving Him along the path. Having arrived, they sleep beneath the shade of safety and remain at peace in the vicinity of the Generous Bestower. One gnostic says: “When dealing [with God] passes into the heart, the limbs come to rest.”<\/p><p>Such rest belongs to realized masters and disciples connected to them. Another group, however, are ignorant people who affect spiritual poverty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المتفقرة الجاهلون<\/bdi>) without a spiritual teacher. Their selves consume their attention: at times they are overcome by them; at times they overcome them. At times the lights of orientation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أنوار التوجه<\/bdi>) shine, and hope of opening (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الفتح<\/bdi>) grows strong; at others the lights are withheld and they think false thoughts of God. They ask whether spiritual opening is possible for them.<\/p><p>Tell them that the whole affair is God's: He brings to Himself whom He wills and keeps distant whom He wills. They conceal defects and harmful inner thoughts which they do not disclose. When opening is delayed and poverty weighs on them, they regret missing worldly ease and say, “Had we any choice, we would not have been killed here by humiliation, poverty and hunger.” Say to them: this was already in God's knowledge and none escapes it; the truthful seeker must be distinguished from the pretender. “Be truthful, and you will find a guide” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كن صادقاً تجد مرشداً<\/bdi>): with sincerity He would have directed you to one who could bring you to Him and relieve the toil. God grants success and guides to the straight path.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"After the grief of spiritual struggle ( المجاهدة ) and the labour of vigilance ( المراقبة ), God sends security into the hearts of those who have arrived and become firmly established ( الواصلون المتمكنون ), and those wayfarers attached to them: peace in witnessing God, and rest in the bodily faculties after the strain of serving Him along the path. Having arrived, they sleep beneath the shade of safety and remain at peace in the vicinity of the Generous Bestower. One gnostic says: “When dealing [with God] passes into the heart, the limbs come to rest.” Such rest belongs to realized masters and disciples connected to them. Another group, however, are ignorant people who affect spiritual poverty ( المتفقرة الجاهلون ) without a spiritual teacher. Their selves consume their attention: at times they are overcome by them; at times they overcome them. At times the lights of orientation ( أنوار التوجه ) shine, and hope of opening ( الفتح ) grows strong; at others the lights are withheld and they think false thoughts of God. They ask whether spiritual opening is possible for them. Tell them that the whole affair is God's: He brings to Himself whom He wills and keeps distant whom He wills. They conceal defects and harmful inner thoughts which they do not disclose. When opening is delayed and poverty weighs on them, they regret missing worldly ease and say, “Had we any choice, we would not have been killed here by humiliation, poverty and hunger.” Say to them: this was already in God's knowledge and none escapes it; the truthful seeker must be distinguished from the pretender. “Be truthful, and you will find a guide” ( كن صادقاً تجد مرشداً ): with sincerity He would have directed you to one who could bring you to Him and relieve the toil. God grants success and guides to the straight path."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Then He named the reason for the flight of those who fled.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Then He named the reason for the flight of those who fled."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C423.1","page":423,"ar":"سبق بيان معنى العبارة عند إشارة الآية / ٢١٢ من سورة البقرة.","en":"The meaning of this expression was explained earlier in the allusion on verse 212 of Sūrat al-Baqara.","anchor":"إذا انتقلت المعاملة إلى القلوب استراحت الجوارح"}],"english_characters":4808,"arabic_characters":4093},{"id":"q3-155","q":"3:155","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:155 · The slip of the archers and forgiveness after withdrawing from a teacher","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 424–424","cairo":"I:424","source_page_first":424,"source_page_last":424,"ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ تَوَلَّوْا مِنْكُمْ يَوْمَ الْتَقَى الْجَمْعانِ إِنَّمَا اسْتَزَلَّهُمُ الشَّيْطانُ بِبَعْضِ ما كَسَبُوا وَلَقَدْ عَفَا اللَّهُ عَنْهُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ حَلِيمٌ (155)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنَّ الَّذِينَ تَوَلَّوْا مِنْكُمْ وانهزموا يوم أحد يَوْمَ الْتَقَى الْجَمْعانِ جمع المسلمين وجمع الكفار إنما كان السبب في انهزامهم أن الشيطان اسْتَزَلَّهُمُ، أي: طلب زللهم فأطاعوه، أي: زين لهم الفرار فأطاعوه، بسبب بعض ما كَسَبُوا من الإثم، كمخالفة أمر النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم، والحرص على الغنيمة، وذنوب اقترفوها قبل الجهاد، فإن المعاصي تجر بعضها بعضاً، كالطاعة، وَلَقَدْ عَفَا اللَّهُ عَنْهُمْ فيما فعلوا من الفرار لتوبتهم واعتذارهم إِنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ للذنوب، حَلِيمٌ لا يعاجل بعقوبة المذنب كي يتوب.\nالإشارة: إن الذين تولوا منكم يا معشر الفقراء، ورجعوا عن صحبة الشيوخ، حين التقى في قلبهم الخصمان:\nخصم يرغبهم في الثبوت، وخصم يدلهم على الرجوع، ثم غلب خصم الرجوع فرجعوا، إنما استزلهم الشيطان بسوء أدبهم، فإن تابوا ورجعوا، أقبلوا عليهم، وقَبل الله توبتهم، وعفا عنهم، فإنه سبحانه غفور حليم.\nثم حذّر من التشبه بالمنافقين فى ضعف اليقين، وما ينشأ عنه من مقالة الجاهلين، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِنَّ الَّذِينَ تَوَلَّوْا مِنْكُمْ يَوْمَ الْتَقَى الْجَمْعانِ إِنَّمَا اسْتَزَلَّهُمُ الشَّيْطانُ بِبَعْضِ ما كَسَبُوا وَلَقَدْ عَفَا اللَّهُ عَنْهُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ غَفُورٌ حَلِيمٌ (155)","quran_en":"155. Those of you who turned back on the day the two hosts met—it was only Satan who made them slip on account of some of what they had earned. Yet God has pardoned them. God is truly Forgiving, Clement.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The causes of the retreat and the delay of punishment","html":"<p>Those who turned back and fled when the two armies met at Uḥud did so because Satan caused them to slip (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">استزلهم<\/bdi>): he sought to make them stumble and they obeyed him. He adorned flight for them because of some of the sins they had committed—disobeying the Prophet, being eager for booty, or offences committed before fighting. Acts of disobedience lead to further disobedience, just as acts of obedience lead to further obedience. Yet God pardoned their flight because they repented and offered their apologies. He forgives sins and is Clement (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حليم<\/bdi>), not hastening a sinner's punishment, so that repentance remains possible.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Those who turned back and fled when the two armies met at Uḥud did so because Satan caused them to slip ( استزلهم ): he sought to make them stumble and they obeyed him. He adorned flight for them because of some of the sins they had committed—disobeying the Prophet, being eager for booty, or offences committed before fighting. Acts of disobedience lead to further disobedience, just as acts of obedience lead to further obedience. Yet God pardoned their flight because they repented and offered their apologies. He forgives sins and is Clement ( حليم ), not hastening a sinner's punishment, so that repentance remains possible."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"A disciple's conflict and restoration through repentance","html":"<p>O spiritual poor, those who turned away from accompanying their teachers did so when two adversaries met in their hearts: one inviting them to remain, another urging return to ordinary pursuits. The latter prevailed and they withdrew. Satan caused their lapse through their poor manners (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">سوء الأدب<\/bdi>). If they repent and return to the teachers, the teachers should welcome them and God accepts their repentance and pardons them, for He is Forgiving, Clement.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"O spiritual poor, those who turned away from accompanying their teachers did so when two adversaries met in their hearts: one inviting them to remain, another urging return to ordinary pursuits. The latter prevailed and they withdrew. Satan caused their lapse through their poor manners ( سوء الأدب ). If they repent and return to the teachers, the teachers should welcome them and God accepts their repentance and pardons them, for He is Forgiving, Clement."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then warned against resembling hypocrites in weakness of certainty and the language arising from it.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then warned against resembling hypocrites in weakness of certainty and the language arising from it."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1190,"arabic_characters":1083},{"id":"q3-156","q":"3:156","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:156 · Death, travel, destiny and the tranquility of acceptance","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 424–425","cairo":"I:424–425","source_page_first":424,"source_page_last":425,"ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تَكُونُوا كَالَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا وَقالُوا لِإِخْوانِهِمْ إِذا ضَرَبُوا فِي الْأَرْضِ أَوْ كانُوا غُزًّى لَوْ كانُوا عِنْدَنا ما ماتُوا وَما قُتِلُوا لِيَجْعَلَ اللَّهُ ذلِكَ حَسْرَةً فِي قُلُوبِهِمْ وَاللَّهُ يُحْيِي وَيُمِيتُ وَاللَّهُ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ بَصِيرٌ (156)\nقلت: (غُزّى) : جمع غازٍ، كعافٍ وعفى، وإنما وضع (إذا) موضع (إذ) لحكاية الحال.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تَكُونُوا كَالَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا ونافقوا، كعبد الله بن أُبي، وأصحابه، وَقالُوا لِإِخْوانِهِمْ في النسب، أو في المذهب، أي: قالوا لأجلهم أو في شأنهم، إِذا ضَرَبُوا فِي الْأَرْضِ أي: سافروا للتجارة أو غيرها فماتوا، أَوْ كانُوا غُزًّى أي: غازين فقتلوا في الغزو: لَوْ كانُوا عِنْدَنا مُقيمين ما ماتُوا وَما قُتِلُوا، وإنما نطقوا بذلك لِيَجْعَلَ اللَّهُ ذلِكَ القول الناشئ عن الاعتقاد الفاسد حَسْرَةً فِي قُلُوبِهِمْ بالاغتمام على ما فات، والتحسر على ما لم يأت، وَاللَّهُ هو يُحْيِي وَيُمِيتُ بلا سبب في الإقامة والسفر، فليس يمنع حذر من قدر، وَاللَّهُ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ، أيها المؤمنون بَصِيرٌ، ففيه تهديد لهم على أن يُماثلوا المنافقين في هذا الاعتقاد الفاسد، ومن قرأ بالياء فهو تهديد لهم. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: لا ينبغي للأقوياء من أهل اليقين أن يتشبهوا بضعفاء اليقين، كانوا علماء أو صالحين أو طالحين، حيث يقولون لإخوانهم إذا سافروا لأرض مخوفة أو بلد الوباء: لو جلسوا عندنا ما ماتوا وما قتلوا، وما دَرَوْا أن الله قدَّر الآجال كما قدَّر الأرزاق وجميع الشئون والأحوال، وعيَّن لها أوقاتاً محدودة في أزله، فكل مقدور يبرز في وقته، «ما من نَفَسٍ تُبديه، إلاَّ وله قَدَر فيك يمضيه» ، فما قدَّره في سابق علمه لا بد أن يكون، وما لم يقدره لا يكون، ولا تجلبه حركة ولا سكون. ولله در القائل:\nمَا لا يقدر لا يكون بحيلة ... أبدا وما هو كائن سيكون\nسيكون ما هو كائن في وقته ... وَأخُو الجَهَالَةِ مُتْعَبٌ مَحْزُونُ\nيَجْرِي الحَريصُ ولا يَنَالُ بِحرْصِهِ ... شَيْئاً ويَحْظَى عَاجِزٌ وَمَهِينٌ\nفَدعَ الهُمُومَ، تَعَرَّ مِنْ أثْوابِهَا، ... إنْ كانَ عِنْدَكَ بالْقَضَاءِ يَقِينُ\nهَوِّنْ عَلَيْكَ وَكُنْ بِرَبِّكَ وَاثِقاً ... فأخو الحَقِيقَةِ شَأنُه التَّهْوِينُ\nوكان سيدنا عمر رضي الله عنه يتمثل بهذه الأبيات:\nفَهَوَّنْ عَلَيْكَ فَإنَّ الأُمُورَ ... بكفِّ الإلَهِ مَقَادِيرُهَا\nفَلَيْسَ يَأتِيكَ مَصْرُوفُهَا ... ولا عَازِبٌ عَنْكَ مَقْدُورُها\nوكل من لم يحقق الإيمان بالقدر لا ينفك عن الحسرة والكدر، ومن أراد النعيم المقيم فليثلج صدره ببرد الرضا والتسليم، ومن أراد الروح والريحان فعليه بجنات العرفان، وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nثم رغّب الحق تعالى فى الموت فى الجهاد، ورجّح الموت مطلقا على الحياة، فقال:","quran_ar":"يا أَيُّهَا الَّذِينَ آمَنُوا لا تَكُونُوا كَالَّذِينَ كَفَرُوا وَقالُوا لِإِخْوانِهِمْ إِذا ضَرَبُوا فِي الْأَرْضِ أَوْ كانُوا غُزًّى لَوْ كانُوا عِنْدَنا ما ماتُوا وَما قُتِلُوا لِيَجْعَلَ اللَّهُ ذلِكَ حَسْرَةً فِي قُلُوبِهِمْ وَاللَّهُ يُحْيِي وَيُمِيتُ وَاللَّهُ بِما تَعْمَلُونَ بَصِيرٌ (156)","quran_en":"156. O you who believe, do not be like those who disbelieved and said of their brothers when they journeyed through the earth or went out to fight, “Had they remained with us, they would not have died or been killed.” Thus God makes this a cause of regret in their hearts. God gives life and causes death, and God sees what you do.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Ghuzzā (غُزّى) and the historical present","html":"<p>I say: <strong>Ghuzzā<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غُزّى<\/bdi>) is the plural of <strong>ghāzī<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غازٍ<\/bdi>, one engaged in fighting), like <strong>ʿāfin/ʿufā<\/strong>. Here <strong>idhā<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إذا<\/bdi>) stands for <strong>idh<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إذ<\/bdi>) to represent a past event as if occurring before us (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حكاية الحال<\/bdi>).<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: Ghuzzā ( غُزّى ) is the plural of ghāzī ( غازٍ , one engaged in fighting), like ʿāfin/ʿufā . Here idhā ( إذا ) stands for idh ( إذ ) to represent a past event as if occurring before us ( حكاية الحال )."},{"type":"exposition","title":"False counterfactuals about travel and death","html":"<p>Do not be like those who disbelieved and practiced hypocrisy, such as ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ubayy and his companions. They spoke concerning their brothers—brothers by kinship or creed, that is, on their behalf or about them—who had travelled for commerce or another purpose and died, or who had gone out on a campaign and been killed: “Had they stayed with us, neither death nor killing would have overtaken them.” Their utterance arises from corrupt belief (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الاعتقاد الفاسد<\/bdi>) and becomes a cause of remorse: grief for what has passed and regret for what never came to be. God gives life and causes death without a cause, whether one stays at home or journeys. No precaution wards off destiny (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لا يمنع حذر من قدر<\/bdi>). He sees your deeds, believers; the statement therefore warns you not to adopt the same mistaken notion. With the reading in the third person (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">بالياء<\/bdi>), the threat is directed towards the hypocrites. God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Do not be like those who disbelieved and practiced hypocrisy, such as ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ubayy and his companions. They spoke concerning their brothers—brothers by kinship or creed, that is, on their behalf or about them—who had travelled for commerce or another purpose and died, or who had gone out on a campaign and been killed: “Had they stayed with us, neither death nor killing would have overtaken them.” Their utterance arises from corrupt belief ( الاعتقاد الفاسد ) and becomes a cause of remorse: grief for what has passed and regret for what never came to be. God gives life and causes death without a cause, whether one stays at home or journeys. No precaution wards off destiny ( لا يمنع حذر من قدر ). He sees your deeds, believers; the statement therefore warns you not to adopt the same mistaken notion. With the reading in the third person ( بالياء ), the threat is directed towards the hypocrites. God knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The predetermined term and the release of anxiety","html":"<p>People strong in certainty should not resemble those weak in certainty—scholars, righteous people or wrongdoers—when they say of brothers travelling to a dangerous country or one afflicted by disease: “Had they remained with us, they would neither have died nor been killed.” They fail to realize that God has determined allotted lifespans as He determined livelihood, affairs and circumstances, and appointed their times in eternity. Every decree emerges at its appointed hour. As the saying has it: “No breath do you exhale but that it contains a decree carried out in you” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ما من نفس تبديه إلا وله قدر فيك يمضيه<\/bdi>). What His prior knowledge has decreed must occur; what He did not decree cannot be brought about by motion or stillness.<\/p><p>The poet says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">What is not decreed no contrivance can bring;<br/>what is to be will certainly be.<br/>What is to be will come at its appointed hour;<br/>only the ignorant are wearied and grieved.<br/>The greedy man runs but gains nothing through greed;<br/>the weak and the lowly may receive their share.<br/>So put away cares; strip off their garments,<br/>if you hold firm certainty in divine decree.<br/>Be at ease and place your trust in your Lord:<br/>the one realized in truth makes matters light.<\/blockquote><p>Our master ʿUmar, God be pleased with him, used to recite these lines:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">Make things easier upon yourself, for their measures<br/>rest in the hand of God.<br/>What He turns from you shall never reach you;<br/>what He decrees for you shall never be far away.<\/blockquote><p>Whoever has not realized faith in decree remains caught in remorse and distress. Whoever seeks enduring delight should cool the breast with acceptance (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الرضا<\/bdi>) and surrender (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التسليم<\/bdi>); whoever seeks ease and sweet repose should enter the gardens of gnosis (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العرفان<\/bdi>). Success is through God, the guide to the straight path.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"People strong in certainty should not resemble those weak in certainty—scholars, righteous people or wrongdoers—when they say of brothers travelling to a dangerous country or one afflicted by disease: “Had they remained with us, they would neither have died nor been killed.” They fail to realize that God has determined allotted lifespans as He determined livelihood, affairs and circumstances, and appointed their times in eternity. Every decree emerges at its appointed hour. As the saying has it: “No breath do you exhale but that it contains a decree carried out in you” ( ما من نفس تبديه إلا وله قدر فيك يمضيه ). What His prior knowledge has decreed must occur; what He did not decree cannot be brought about by motion or stillness. The poet says: What is not decreed no contrivance can bring; what is to be will certainly be. What is to be will come at its appointed hour; only the ignorant are wearied and grieved. The greedy man runs but gains nothing through greed; the weak and the lowly may receive their share. So put away cares; strip off their garments, if you hold firm certainty in divine decree. Be at ease and place your trust in your Lord: the one realized in truth makes matters light. Our master ʿUmar, God be pleased with him, used to recite these lines: Make things easier upon yourself, for their measures rest in the hand of God. What He turns from you shall never reach you; what He decrees for you shall never be far away. Whoever has not realized faith in decree remains caught in remorse and distress. Whoever seeks enduring delight should cool the breast with acceptance ( الرضا ) and surrender ( التسليم ); whoever seeks ease and sweet repose should enter the gardens of gnosis ( العرفان ). Success is through God, the guide to the straight path."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>He then encouraged them concerning death in striving, and preferred death more generally to worldly life.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"He then encouraged them concerning death in striving, and preferred death more generally to worldly life."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":3017,"arabic_characters":2429},{"id":"q3-157-158","q":"3:157–158","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:157–158 · Death in God's way and the spiritual gain of meeting Him","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 425–426","cairo":"I:425–426","source_page_first":425,"source_page_last":426,"ar":"وَلَئِنْ قُتِلْتُمْ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ أَوْ مُتُّمْ لَمَغْفِرَةٌ مِنَ اللَّهِ وَرَحْمَةٌ خَيْرٌ مِمَّا يَجْمَعُونَ (157) وَلَئِنْ مُتُّمْ أَوْ قُتِلْتُمْ لَإِلَى اللَّهِ تُحْشَرُونَ (158)\nقلت: إذا اجتمع القسم والشرط ذكر جواب الأول وأغنى عن الثاني، فقوله: (لمغفرة) : جواب القسم، أغنى عن جواب (إن) ، والتقدير: إن قتلتم في سبيل الله غفر الله لكم، ثم سد عنه (لمغفرة..) الخ، ومن قرأ: (مِتم) بكسر الميم، فهو من: مات يمات، كهاب يهاب هِبتُ، وخاف يخاف خِفتُ، ومن قرأ بالضم: فمن مات يموت، كقال يقول قُلت.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إن السفر والغزو ليس هما مما يجلب الموت أو يقدم الأجل، وعلى تقدير: لو وقع ذلك وحضر أجلكم فيه وقتلتم فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ بالسيف، أَوْ مُتُّمْ حتف أنفكم، لما تنالون من المغفرة والرحمة والروح والريحان خَيْرٌ مِمَّا يَجْمَعُونَ من حطام الدنيا الفانية لو لم تموتوا، وعلى أي وجه متم أو قتلتم فلا تحشرون إلا إلى الله، لا إلى أحد غيره، فيوفى جزاءكم ويعظم ثوابكم، وأما البقاء في الدنيا فلا مطمع لأحد فيه، سافر أو قعد في بيته، وقدَّم أولاً القتل على الموت وأخره ثانياً لأن الأول رتب عليه المغفرة والرحمة، وهما في حق من قتل في الجهاد أعظم ممن مات بغيره، فقدمه اعتناء به، وفي الثاني رتب عليه الحشر، وهو مستوٍ في القتل والموت، فلا مزية فيه للقتل على الموت. والله أعلم.\nالإشارة: ولئن قتلتم نفوسكم وبذلتم مهجكم في طلب محبوبكم، فظفرتم بالوصول إليه قبل موتكم، أو متم في السير قبل الوصول إلى محبوبكم، لما تنالون من كمال اليقين وشهود رب العالمين، أو من المغفرة والرحمة التي تضمكم إلى جواره، خير مما كنتم تجمعون من الدنيا قبل توجهكم إليه، فإن الموت والحشر مكتوب على كل مخلوق، فيظهر فوز المجاهدين والمتوجهين، وغبن القاعدين المتسوفين. وبالله التوفيق.\nولمّا وقع ما وقع يوم أحد من مخالفة الرسول والفرار عنه- عليه الصلاة والسلام- لم يعاتب صلّى الله عليه وسلم أحدا، ولكن ألان لهم الكلام وعفا عنهم، كما أخبر عن ذلك الحق تعالى بقوله:","quran_ar":"وَلَئِنْ قُتِلْتُمْ فِي سَبِيلِ اللَّهِ أَوْ مُتُّمْ لَمَغْفِرَةٌ مِنَ اللَّهِ وَرَحْمَةٌ خَيْرٌ مِمَّا يَجْمَعُونَ (157) وَلَئِنْ مُتُّمْ أَوْ قُتِلْتُمْ لَإِلَى اللَّهِ تُحْشَرُونَ (158)","quran_en":"157. If you are killed in God's way or die, then forgiveness and mercy from God are better than what they gather. 158. And whether you die or are killed, to God you shall surely be gathered.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"When an oath precedes a conditional","html":"<p>I say: When an oath (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قسم<\/bdi>) and a condition (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شرط<\/bdi>) occur together, the answer to the first suffices for the second. Thus “forgiveness” (<strong>la-maghfira<\/strong>, <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لَمَغْفِرَة<\/bdi>) is the answer to the oath (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جواب القسم<\/bdi>) and takes the place of the answer to <strong>in<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">إن<\/bdi>): “If you are killed in God's way, God will forgive you,” with the express formula “forgiveness ... is better” standing in its place. One reading of “you die” has kasra on the <strong>mīm<\/strong> (<strong>mittum<\/strong>, <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مِتُّم<\/bdi>), from <strong>māta/yamātu<\/strong>, like <strong>hāba/yahābu/hibtu<\/strong> and <strong>khāfa/yakhāfu/khiftu<\/strong>; another has ḍamma (<strong>muttum<\/strong>, <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مُتُّم<\/bdi>), from <strong>māta/yamūtu<\/strong>, like <strong>qāla/yaqūlu/qultu<\/strong>.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: When an oath ( قسم ) and a condition ( شرط ) occur together, the answer to the first suffices for the second. Thus “forgiveness” ( la-maghfira , لَمَغْفِرَة ) is the answer to the oath ( جواب القسم ) and takes the place of the answer to in ( إن ): “If you are killed in God's way, God will forgive you,” with the express formula “forgiveness ... is better” standing in its place. One reading of “you die” has kasra on the mīm ( mittum , مِتُّم ), from māta/yamātu , like hāba/yahābu/hibtu and khāfa/yakhāfu/khiftu ; another has ḍamma ( muttum , مُتُّم ), from māta/yamūtu , like qāla/yaqūlu/qultu ."},{"type":"exposition","title":"Why killing is named first, and why death follows","html":"<p>Travel and battle neither cause death nor advance its appointed term. But if your term comes during striving and you are killed by the sword in God's cause—or if you die naturally—the forgiveness, mercy, repose and delight awaiting you are better than the perishable worldly gains they might have accumulated had they lived. In either case, death or killing, you are gathered to God alone, who completes your recompense and magnifies your reward. No one, traveller or stay-at-home, can hope to remain forever in the world.<\/p><p>God places being killed before ordinary death in the first verse because forgiveness and mercy are promised in a special measure to the one killed in divine striving, so killing is advanced in honour of that promise. In the second verse, where the outcome is simply resurrection and gathering, death is named first because there is no difference between the two ways of dying with respect to that destiny. God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Travel and battle neither cause death nor advance its appointed term. But if your term comes during striving and you are killed by the sword in God's cause—or if you die naturally—the forgiveness, mercy, repose and delight awaiting you are better than the perishable worldly gains they might have accumulated had they lived. In either case, death or killing, you are gathered to God alone, who completes your recompense and magnifies your reward. No one, traveller or stay-at-home, can hope to remain forever in the world. God places being killed before ordinary death in the first verse because forgiveness and mercy are promised in a special measure to the one killed in divine striving, so killing is advanced in honour of that promise. In the second verse, where the outcome is simply resurrection and gathering, death is named first because there is no difference between the two ways of dying with respect to that destiny. God knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The fruit of dying to the self before bodily death","html":"<p>If you kill your lower selves (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أنفسكم<\/bdi>), offering your lives in seeking the Beloved, and achieve arrival (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الوصول<\/bdi>) before death—or die during wayfaring (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">السير<\/bdi>) before arriving—the perfection of certainty (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">كمال اليقين<\/bdi>) and witnessing of the Lord of the Worlds that you attain, or the forgiveness and mercy which bring you near Him, are better than the worldly possessions gathered before you turned to Him. Death and resurrection are decreed for every creature; then the success of those who struggled and turned towards God, and the loss of those who delayed and remained behind, will become plain. Success is through God.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"If you kill your lower selves ( أنفسكم ), offering your lives in seeking the Beloved, and achieve arrival ( الوصول ) before death—or die during wayfaring ( السير ) before arriving—the perfection of certainty ( كمال اليقين ) and witnessing of the Lord of the Worlds that you attain, or the forgiveness and mercy which bring you near Him, are better than the worldly possessions gathered before you turned to Him. Death and resurrection are decreed for every creature; then the success of those who struggled and turned towards God, and the loss of those who delayed and remained behind, will become plain. Success is through God."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Despite what occurred at Uḥud—the opposition to the Messenger and the flight—he did not reproach the guilty, but addressed them gently and pardoned them, as God declares.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Despite what occurred at Uḥud—the opposition to the Messenger and the flight—he did not reproach the guilty, but addressed them gently and pardoned them, as God declares."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2347,"arabic_characters":1721},{"id":"q3-159","q":"3:159","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:159 · Gentleness, consultation and the three degrees of trust","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 426–428","cairo":"I:426–428","source_page_first":426,"source_page_last":428,"ar":"فَبِما رَحْمَةٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ لِنْتَ لَهُمْ وَلَوْ كُنْتَ فَظًّا غَلِيظَ الْقَلْبِ لانْفَضُّوا مِنْ حَوْلِكَ فَاعْفُ عَنْهُمْ وَاسْتَغْفِرْ لَهُمْ وَشاوِرْهُمْ فِي الْأَمْرِ فَإِذا عَزَمْتَ فَتَوَكَّلْ عَلَى اللَّهِ إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ الْمُتَوَكِّلِينَ (159)\nقلت: (فبما) : صلة. والفظ: الجافي، يقال: فظ فظاظةً وفظوظاً، ورجل فظ، وامرأة فظة، والفض- بغير المشالة: التفرق، ويطلق على الكسر، ومنه: لا يفضض الله فاك.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: فبرحمة من الله ونعمة كنت سهلاً ليناً رفيقاً، فحين عصوا أمرك، وفروا عنك، ألنت لهم جانبك، ورفقت بهم، بل اغتممت من أجلهم مما أصابهم، وَلَوْ كُنْتَ فَظًّا جافيا سيئ الخلق غَلِيظَ الْقَلْبِ قاسيَهُ فأغلظت لهم القول، لَانْفَضُّوا مِنْ حَوْلِكَ أي: لتفرقوا عنك، ولم يسكنوا إليك، فَاعْفُ عَنْهُمْ فيما يختص بك، وَاسْتَغْفِرْ لَهُمْ في حق ربك حتى يشفعك فيهم، وَشاوِرْهُمْ فِي الْأَمْرِ الذي يصح أن يشاور فيه تطييباً لخاطرهم، ورفعاً لأقدارهم، واستخراجاً وتمهيداً لسنة المشاورة لغيرهم، وخصوصاً الأمراء.\nقال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «ما شقى عبد بمشورة، وما سعد باستغناء برأى» . وقال أيضاً: «مَا خَابَ مَن اسْتَخَارَ، وَلاَ نَدِمَ من اسْتَشَار» . وقال أيضا- عليه الصلاة والسلام- «إذَا كَانَ أُمَرَاؤُكُمْ خِيَارَكُمْ، وَأَغْنِيَاؤُكُمْ أَسخيَاءَكُمْ، وأَمرُكُم شُورَى بَيْنَكُم، فَظَهْرُ الأرْض خَيْرٌ لَكُمْ مِنْ بَطْنِهَا. وإذا كَانَ أُمَرَاؤُكُمْ شِرَارَكُمْ وأَغْنِيَاؤُكُمْ بُخَلاءَكُمْ، ولم تكن أموركم شُورَى بَيْنَكُم، فبَطْنُ الأرْضِ خَيرٌ من ظَهْرِهَا» .\nفَإِذا عَزَمْتَ على شيء بعد الشورى، (فتوكل على الله) أي: ثق به وكيلاً، إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ الْمُتَوَكِّلِينَ فينصرهم ويهديهم إلى ما فيه صلاحهم.\nالإشارة: ما اتصف به نبينا- عليه الصلاة والسلام- من السهولة والليونة والرفق بالأمة، اتصفت به ورثته من الأولياء العارفين، والعلماء الراسخين، ليتهيأ لهم الدعوة إلى الله، أو إلى أحكام الله، ولو كانوا فظاظاً غلاظاً لانفض الناس من حولهم، ولم يتهيأ لهم تعريف ولا تعليم، فينبغي لهم أن يعفوا ويصفحوا ويغفروا ويصبروا على جفوة الناس، ويستغفروا لهم، ويشاوروهم في أمورهم، اقتداء برسولهم، فإذا عزموا على إمضاء شيء فليتوكلوا على الله إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ الْمُتَوَكِّلِينَ.\nقال الجنيد- رضي الله عنه-: (التوكل أن تقبل بالكلية على ربك وتعرض عمن دونه) . وقال الثوري: أن تفني تدبيرك في تدبيره، وترضى بالله وكيلاً ومدبراً، قال الله تعالى: وَكَفى بِاللَّهِ وَكِيلًا. وقال ذو النون: (خُلع الأرباب، وقطع الأسباب.) وقال الخواص: قطع الخوف والرجاء مما سوى الله تعالى. وقال العرجي: رد العيش إلى يوم واحد، وإسقاط هم غد. هـ. وقال سهل: معرفة معطي أرزاق المخلوقين، ولا يصح لأحد التوكل حتى تكون عنده السماء كالصفر (١) والأرض كالحديد، لا ينزل من السماء قطر، ولا يخرج من الأرض نبات، ويعلم أن الله لا ينسى له ما ضمن من رزقه بين هذين. هـ. وقيل: هو اكتفاء العبد الذليل بالرب الجليل، كاكتفاء الخليل بالخليل، حين لم ينظر إلى عناية جبريل. وقيل لبهلول المجنون: متى يكون العبد متوكلاً؟ قال: إذا كان بالنفس غريباً بين الخلق، وبالقلب قريباً إلى الحق.\nوقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «مَنْ سَرَّهُ أنْ يَكُونَ أكْرَم النَّاسِ فَلْيَتقِ الله، ومَنْ سَرَّه أن يكُون أغنى النّاس فليكن بما في يد الله أوثق منه بما في يَده» .\nقال ابن جزي: التوكل هو الاعتماد على الله في تحصيل المنافع وحفظها بعد حصولها، وفي دفع المضرات ورفعها بعد وقوعها، وهو من أعلى المقامات، لوجهين: أحدهما: قوله: إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ الْمُتَوَكِّلِينَ، والآخر:\nالضمان الذي في قوله: وَمَنْ يَتَوَكَّلْ عَلَى اللَّهِ فَهُوَ حَسْبُهُ، وقد يكون واجباً لقوله: وَعَلَى اللَّهِ فَتَوَكَّلُوا إِنْ كُنْتُمْ مُؤْمِنِينَ، فجعله شرطاً في الإيمان، ولظاهر قوله: وَعَلَى اللَّهِ فَلْيَتَوَكَّلِ الْمُؤْمِنُونَ فإن الأمر محمول على الوجوب.\nواعلم أن الناس في التوكل على ثلاث مراتب:\nالأولى: أن يعتمد العبد على ربه، كاعتماد الإنسان على وكيله المأمون عنده، الذي لا يشك فى نصيحته له وقيامه بمصالحه. الثانية: أن يكون العبد مع ربه كالطفل مع أمه لا يعرف سواها ولا يلجأ إِلاَّ إليها. الثالثة: أن يكون العبد مع ربه كالميت بين يدي الغاسل، قد أسلم إليه نفسه بالكلية.\nفصاحب الدرجة الأولى عنده حظ من النظر لنفسه، بخلاف صاحب الثانية. وصاحب الثانية له حظ من الاختيار، بخلاف صاحب الثالثة. وهذه الدرجات مبنية على التوحيد الخاص، الذي تكلمتُ عليه في قوله:\nوَإِلهُكُمْ إِلهٌ واحِدٌ، فهي تقوى بقوته وتضعف بضعفه.\nفإن قيل: هل يشترط في التوكل ترك الأسباب أم لا؟ فالجواب: أن الأسباب على ثلاثة أقسام:\nأحدها: سبب معلوم قطعاً قد أجراه الله، فهذا لا يجوز تركه كالأكل لرفع الجوع واللباس لرفع البرد.\nالثاني: سبب مظنون: كالتجارة وطلب المعاش، وشبه ذلك، فهذا لا يقدح فعله في التوكل، فإن التوكل من أعمال القلوب لا من أعمال البدن، ويجوز تركه لمن قوي عليه.\nوالثالث: سبب موهوم بعيد، فهذا يقدح فعله في التوكل، قلت: ولعل هذا مثل طلب الكيمياء والكنوز وعلم النار والسحر، وشبه ذلك.\nثم فوق التوكل التفويض، وهو: الاستسلام لأمر الله تعالى بالكلية، فإن المتوكل له مراد واختيار، وهو يطلب مراده في الاعتماد على ربه، وأما المفوض فليس له مراد ولا اختيار، بل أسند الاختيار إلى الله تعالى، فهو أكمل أدباً مع الله. هـ وأصله للغزالي، وسيأتي بقية الكلام عند قوله: وَتَوَكَّلْ عَلَى الْحَيِّ الَّذِي لا يَمُوتُ. وبالله التوفيق.\nولما أمر نبيّه- عليه الصلاة والسلام- بالتوكل، رغب فيه جميع عباده، فقال:","quran_ar":"فَبِما رَحْمَةٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ لِنْتَ لَهُمْ وَلَوْ كُنْتَ فَظًّا غَلِيظَ الْقَلْبِ لانْفَضُّوا مِنْ حَوْلِكَ فَاعْفُ عَنْهُمْ وَاسْتَغْفِرْ لَهُمْ وَشاوِرْهُمْ فِي الْأَمْرِ فَإِذا عَزَمْتَ فَتَوَكَّلْ عَلَى اللَّهِ إِنَّ اللَّهَ يُحِبُّ الْمُتَوَكِّلِينَ (159)","quran_en":"159. It was by a mercy from God that you were gentle with them. Had you been harsh and hard of heart, they would have scattered from around you. So pardon them, seek forgiveness for them, and consult them in the affair. Then, when you have resolved, trust in God; truly God loves those who trust in Him.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Fabi-mā, faẓẓ and faḍḍ","html":"<p>I say: The <strong>mā<\/strong> of <strong>fa-bi-mā<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فَبِمَا<\/bdi>) is additional for emphasis (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صلة<\/bdi>). <strong>Faẓẓ<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">فظّ<\/bdi>) means coarse or rough; its noun forms include <strong>faẓāẓa<\/strong> and <strong>fuẓūẓ<\/strong>. A man may be called <strong>faẓẓ<\/strong> and a woman <strong>faẓẓa<\/strong>. <strong>Faḍḍ<\/strong>, written with <strong>ḍād<\/strong> (ض) rather than <strong>ẓāʾ<\/strong> (ظ), means dispersal, and may also mean breaking, whence the blessing “May God not break your mouth.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: The mā of fa-bi-mā ( فَبِمَا ) is additional for emphasis ( صلة ). Faẓẓ ( فظّ ) means coarse or rough; its noun forms include faẓāẓa and fuẓūẓ . A man may be called faẓẓ and a woman faẓẓa . Faḍḍ , written with ḍād (ض) rather than ẓāʾ (ظ), means dispersal, and may also mean breaking, whence the blessing “May God not break your mouth.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Forgiveness, esteem and prudent decision","html":"<p>By divine mercy and favour you were gentle, approachable and kind. When they disobeyed and fled, you still showed kindness, indeed feeling grief on their account. Had you been coarse in manners and hard-hearted, speaking harshly, they would have dispersed and found no ease with you. Pardon their wrong towards you; seek God's forgiveness for what concerns His right, so that your intercession might benefit them; and consult them in matters legitimately open to consultation. This consoles them, honours their judgment, draws counsel from them and establishes a precedent of consultation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المشاورة<\/bdi>) for others, especially rulers.<\/p><p>The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, is quoted as saying: “No servant is made wretched by consultation, nor happy by relying solely upon his own opinion.” He also said: “One who seeks guidance [by istikhāra] does not fail, and one who consults does not regret.” He also said: “When your rulers are the best among you, your wealthy people are the generous among you, and your affairs are settled by consultation among you, the surface of the earth is better for you than its interior. When your rulers are the worst among you, your wealthy people are the misers among you, and your affairs are not settled by consultation among you, the earth’s interior is better than its surface.” After consulting, once you resolve upon a course, trust in God as your protecting Agent (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">وكيل<\/bdi>), who loves those exercising trust (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المتوكلين<\/bdi>), guides them and grants what benefits them.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"By divine mercy and favour you were gentle, approachable and kind. When they disobeyed and fled, you still showed kindness, indeed feeling grief on their account. Had you been coarse in manners and hard-hearted, speaking harshly, they would have dispersed and found no ease with you. Pardon their wrong towards you; seek God's forgiveness for what concerns His right, so that your intercession might benefit them; and consult them in matters legitimately open to consultation. This consoles them, honours their judgment, draws counsel from them and establishes a precedent of consultation ( المشاورة ) for others, especially rulers. The Prophet, blessings and peace be upon him, is quoted as saying: “No servant is made wretched by consultation, nor happy by relying solely upon his own opinion.” He also said: “One who seeks guidance [by istikhāra] does not fail, and one who consults does not regret.” He also said: “When your rulers are the best among you, your wealthy people are the generous among you, and your affairs are settled by consultation among you, the surface of the earth is better for you than its interior. When your rulers are the worst among you, your wealthy people are the misers among you, and your affairs are not settled by consultation among you, the earth’s interior is better than its surface.” After consulting, once you resolve upon a course, trust in God as your protecting Agent ( وكيل ), who loves those exercising trust ( المتوكلين ), guides them and grants what benefits them."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The inheritance of Prophetic gentleness and the meaning of tawakkul","html":"<p>The realized friends of God and firmly rooted scholars inherit our Prophet's gentleness and consideration, so that they may summon people to God or teach His rulings. If they were coarse and hard, people would scatter and neither making God known (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تعريف<\/bdi>) nor teaching (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تعليم<\/bdi>) would succeed. They must therefore pardon and forgive, bear others' harshness patiently, ask pardon on their behalf, consult them in their affairs and, when resolved, put their trust in God.<\/p><p>Al-Junayd says: “Trust is to turn entirely to your Lord and turn away from all beneath Him.” Al-Thawrī calls it annihilating one's own management (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">تدبير<\/bdi>) within His management and accepting God as Agent and Director of affairs. God says: “God suffices as a Trustee.” Dhū al-Nūn speaks of casting off other lords and cutting away secondary causes; al-Khawwāṣ, of severing fear and hope with regard to everything but God; al-ʿArjī, of reducing one's concern for subsistence to the present day and putting aside tomorrow's care. Sahl defines it as knowing who provides all created beings. One cannot realize trust, he says, until one could see the heavens as copper and the earth as iron—no rain descending and no growth emerging—and still know that God will not neglect the livelihood He has guaranteed. Another definition is the humble servant's sufficiency in the Majestic Lord, like Abraham's sufficiency in his intimate Friend when he did not look to Gabriel's offer. Asked when the servant trusts, Buhlūl replied: “When he is a stranger to creatures in himself, but near to the Real in his heart.”<\/p><p>The Prophet is quoted as saying: “Whoever wishes to be the noblest of people should fear God; whoever wishes to be the richest should be more confident in what lies with God than in what lies in his own hand.” <a class=\"noteref\" href=\"#/note/XIV-N145\">[XIV-N145]<\/a><\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"The realized friends of God and firmly rooted scholars inherit our Prophet's gentleness and consideration, so that they may summon people to God or teach His rulings. If they were coarse and hard, people would scatter and neither making God known ( تعريف ) nor teaching ( تعليم ) would succeed. They must therefore pardon and forgive, bear others' harshness patiently, ask pardon on their behalf, consult them in their affairs and, when resolved, put their trust in God. Al-Junayd says: “Trust is to turn entirely to your Lord and turn away from all beneath Him.” Al-Thawrī calls it annihilating one's own management ( تدبير ) within His management and accepting God as Agent and Director of affairs. God says: “God suffices as a Trustee.” Dhū al-Nūn speaks of casting off other lords and cutting away secondary causes; al-Khawwāṣ, of severing fear and hope with regard to everything but God; al-ʿArjī, of reducing one's concern for subsistence to the present day and putting aside tomorrow's care. Sahl defines it as knowing who provides all created beings. One cannot realize trust, he says, until one could see the heavens as copper and the earth as iron—no rain descending and no growth emerging—and still know that God will not neglect the livelihood He has guaranteed. Another definition is the humble servant's sufficiency in the Majestic Lord, like Abraham's sufficiency in his intimate Friend when he did not look to Gabriel's offer. Asked when the servant trusts, Buhlūl replied: “When he is a stranger to creatures in himself, but near to the Real in his heart.” The Prophet is quoted as saying: “Whoever wishes to be the noblest of people should fear God; whoever wishes to be the richest should be more confident in what lies with God than in what lies in his own hand.” [XIV-N145]"},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Three degrees, three sorts of means and tafwīḍ beyond tawakkul","html":"<p>Ibn Juzayy explains that <strong>tawakkul<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التوكل<\/bdi>) is reliance upon God in obtaining and preserving benefits, and repelling or removing harms. It is among the highest stations (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المقامات<\/bdi>), as God loves those who rely upon Him [Q 3:159] and guarantees sufficiency to whoever does so [Q 65:3]. It may even be obligatory, because God links it to faith [Q 5:23], and the scriptural imperative indicates an obligation.<\/p><p>There are three degrees. First, a servant relies on the Lord as a man on a trusted attorney who can be counted upon to protect his interests. Second, he is with God like a child with its mother, knowing and seeking refuge in none besides her. Third, he is with God like a corpse before the washer of the dead, having surrendered himself wholly. The first still notices his own interest, unlike the second; the second retains something of personal choice, unlike the third. These stages rest upon particular or realized tawḥīd (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التوحيد الخاص<\/bdi>), previously discussed at Q 2:163, and grow or decline with its strength.<\/p><p>Must reliance entail abandoning outward causes (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأسباب<\/bdi>)? No single answer applies. Causes are of three kinds. First are means known with certainty through God's established order: these may not be abandoned, like eating to relieve hunger or dressing against cold. Second are probable means, such as trading or seeking livelihood: using them does not impair trust, which belongs to the acts of the heart, not to bodily acts; someone strong enough may also leave them. Third are remote, imaginary means (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأسباب الموهومة البعيدة<\/bdi>): pursuing these impairs tawakkul. Ibn ʿAjība suggests searches for alchemy, buried treasure, divinatory “knowledge of fire” or sorcery as examples.<\/p><p>Above tawakkul lies <strong>tafwīḍ<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التفويض<\/bdi>), consigning the whole affair to God in utter surrender. The one who trusts still has an aim and choice, asking God to realize that aim; the one who consigns has no preference or choice, having entrusted choice itself to God. Such a person displays more complete <strong>adab<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">أدب<\/bdi>) towards God. Ibn ʿAjība attributes the underlying account to al-Ghazālī and refers readers to Q 25:58 for further treatment. Success comes from God.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Ibn Juzayy explains that tawakkul ( التوكل ) is reliance upon God in obtaining and preserving benefits, and repelling or removing harms. It is among the highest stations ( المقامات ), as God loves those who rely upon Him [Q 3:159] and guarantees sufficiency to whoever does so [Q 65:3]. It may even be obligatory, because God links it to faith [Q 5:23], and the scriptural imperative indicates an obligation. There are three degrees. First, a servant relies on the Lord as a man on a trusted attorney who can be counted upon to protect his interests. Second, he is with God like a child with its mother, knowing and seeking refuge in none besides her. Third, he is with God like a corpse before the washer of the dead, having surrendered himself wholly. The first still notices his own interest, unlike the second; the second retains something of personal choice, unlike the third. These stages rest upon particular or realized tawḥīd ( التوحيد الخاص ), previously discussed at Q 2:163, and grow or decline with its strength. Must reliance entail abandoning outward causes ( الأسباب )? No single answer applies. Causes are of three kinds. First are means known with certainty through God's established order: these may not be abandoned, like eating to relieve hunger or dressing against cold. Second are probable means, such as trading or seeking livelihood: using them does not impair trust, which belongs to the acts of the heart, not to bodily acts; someone strong enough may also leave them. Third are remote, imaginary means ( الأسباب الموهومة البعيدة ): pursuing these impairs tawakkul. Ibn ʿAjība suggests searches for alchemy, buried treasure, divinatory “knowledge of fire” or sorcery as examples. Above tawakkul lies tafwīḍ ( التفويض ), consigning the whole affair to God in utter surrender. The one who trusts still has an aim and choice, asking God to realize that aim; the one who consigns has no preference or choice, having entrusted choice itself to God. Such a person displays more complete adab ( أدب ) towards God. Ibn ʿAjība attributes the underlying account to al-Ghazālī and refers readers to Q 25:58 for further treatment. Success comes from God."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Having commanded His Prophet to trust, God commends that trust to all His servants.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Having commanded His Prophet to trust, God commends that trust to all His servants."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C427.1","page":427,"ar":"الصفر: النحاس.","en":"Ṣufr means copper.","anchor":"السماء كالصفر"}],"english_characters":5900,"arabic_characters":4770},{"id":"q3-160","q":"3:160","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:160 · Victory, reliance and the defeat of the lower self","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 428–429","cairo":"I:428–429","source_page_first":428,"source_page_last":429,"ar":"إِنْ يَنْصُرْكُمُ اللَّهُ فَلا غالِبَ لَكُمْ وَإِنْ يَخْذُلْكُمْ فَمَنْ ذَا الَّذِي يَنْصُرُكُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِهِ وَعَلَى اللَّهِ فَلْيَتَوَكَّلِ الْمُؤْمِنُونَ (160)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: (إِنْ يَنْصُرْكُمُ اللَّهُ) كما نصركم يوم بدر، (فلا غالب لكم) من أحد من الناس، (وإن يخذلكم) كما خذلكم يوم أحد، (فمن) هذا (الذي ينصركم من بعده) تعالى، أي: فلا ناصر سواه. وهذا تنبيه على الحث على التوكل، وتحريض على ما يستوجب به النصر، وهو الاعتماد على الله، وتحذير مما يستوجب الخذلان، وهو مخالفة أمره وعصيان رسوله، أو الاعتماد على غيره، ولذلك قال: (وعلى الله فليتوكَّل المؤمنون) لِمَا علموا ألا ناصر سواه.\nالإشارة: إن ينصركم الله على مجاهدة النفوس، ودوام السير إلى حضرة القدوس، فلا غالب لكم من النفس، ولا من الناس ولا من الهوى ولا من الشيطان، وإن يخذلكم- والعياذ بالله- فمن ذا الذي ينصركم من بعد خذلانه لكم؟ فليعتمد المريد في سيره على مولاه، وليستنصر به في قطع حظوظه وهواه، فإنه لا ناصر له سواه. وأنشدوا:\nإِذَا كَانَ عُوْنُ اللهِ لِلمَرْءِ نَاصِراً ... تَهَيَّأ لَهُ مِنْ كُلِّ صَعْبِ مُرَادُهُ\nوَإِنْ لَمْ يَكُنْ عَوْنٌ مِن اللهِ للْفَتَى ... فَأَكْثَرُ مَا يَجْنِي عَلَيْهِ اجْتِهَادُهُ\nوبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nولما تبادرت الرماة إلى الغنيمة كما تقدم، وقع فى وهمهم أنه- عليه الصلاة والسلام- يحرمهم من الغنيمة، وذلك غلول لا يليق بحاله- عليه الصلاة والسلام-، فنزه الله نبيه عن ذلك، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِنْ يَنْصُرْكُمُ اللَّهُ فَلا غالِبَ لَكُمْ وَإِنْ يَخْذُلْكُمْ فَمَنْ ذَا الَّذِي يَنْصُرُكُمْ مِنْ بَعْدِهِ وَعَلَى اللَّهِ فَلْيَتَوَكَّلِ الْمُؤْمِنُونَ (160)","quran_en":"160. If God helps you, none can overcome you; but if He forsakes you, who can help you after Him? In God alone, then, let the believers put their trust.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"The conditional structure of divine assistance","html":"<p>If God helps you, as at Badr, no human being can defeat you. If He leaves you without help, as at Uḥud, who besides Him can grant victory? There is no helper apart from Him. The verse encourages reliance (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التوكل<\/bdi>), and the conduct that brings help—dependence upon God—while warning against what leads to being forsaken: disobeying His command, opposing His Messenger or relying upon another. Therefore, “Upon God let the believers place their trust,” for they know no protector besides Him.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"If God helps you, as at Badr, no human being can defeat you. If He leaves you without help, as at Uḥud, who besides Him can grant victory? There is no helper apart from Him. The verse encourages reliance ( التوكل ), and the conduct that brings help—dependence upon God—while warning against what leads to being forsaken: disobeying His command, opposing His Messenger or relying upon another. Therefore, “Upon God let the believers place their trust,” for they know no protector besides Him."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Seeking assistance to overcome the self","html":"<p>If God supports you in struggling against your lower selves (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مجاهدة النفوس<\/bdi>) and constantly travelling towards the Presence of the Most Holy, neither self, fellow human, passion nor Satan can master you. But if He forsakes you—God preserve us—who could help after that? The disciple must depend on his Lord and ask His support in severing self-interest and passion, for no other supporter exists. As the poet says:<\/p><blockquote class=\"poetry\">When God's help becomes a man's supporter,<br/>all difficulty is made ready for his desire.<br/>If God's help does not support a young man,<br/>his own exertion is what harms him most.<\/blockquote><p>Success is through God, the guide to the straight path.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"If God supports you in struggling against your lower selves ( مجاهدة النفوس ) and constantly travelling towards the Presence of the Most Holy, neither self, fellow human, passion nor Satan can master you. But if He forsakes you—God preserve us—who could help after that? The disciple must depend on his Lord and ask His support in severing self-interest and passion, for no other supporter exists. As the poet says: When God's help becomes a man's supporter, all difficulty is made ready for his desire. If God's help does not support a young man, his own exertion is what harms him most. Success is through God, the guide to the straight path."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>When the archers hastened toward the spoils, they suspected that the Prophet might deny them their share, an accusation of <strong>ghulūl<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">غلول<\/bdi>) incompatible with Prophethood. God cleared His Prophet of it.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When the archers hastened toward the spoils, they suspected that the Prophet might deny them their share, an accusation of ghulūl ( غلول ) incompatible with Prophethood. God cleared His Prophet of it."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1335,"arabic_characters":1289},{"id":"q3-161","q":"3:161","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:161 · The accusation of ghulūl, variant readings and the rights of prophets","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 429–430","cairo":"I:429–430","source_page_first":429,"source_page_last":430,"ar":"وَما كانَ لِنَبِيٍّ أَنْ يَغُلَّ وَمَنْ يَغْلُلْ يَأْتِ بِما غَلَّ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ ثُمَّ تُوَفَّى كُلُّ نَفْسٍ مَّا كَسَبَتْ وَهُمْ لا يُظْلَمُونَ (161)\nقلت: الغلول: السرقة من الغنائم، فمن قرأ بفتح الياء وضم الغين، فمعناه: لا ينبغي له أن يأخذ شيئاً من الغنيمة خفية، والمراد: تبرئة رسوله- عليه الصلاة والسلام- من ذلك. ومَن قرأ بضم الياء ففيه وجهان: أحدهما:\nأن يكون المعنى، ما كان لنبي أن يُخان، أي: أن تخونه أُمّتُه في المغانم، وكذلك الأمراء، وإنما خص النبيَّ صلى الله عليه وسلم بذلك لبشاعة ذلك مع النبيّ لأن المعاصي تعظم بحضرته، والثاني: أن يكون المعنى: ما كان لنبي أن يُنسب إلى الخيانة كقوله: فَإِنَّهُمْ لاَ يُكَذِّبُونَكَ أي: لا ينسبونك إلى الكذب.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: ما كانَ ينبغي لِنَبِيٍّ أَنْ يَغُلَّ ويأخذ شيئاً من الغنيمة خفية لأن ذلك خيانة والنبوة تنافي ذلك، والمراد: نزاهة الرسول- عليه الصلاة والسلام- عن ذلك، كقوله: مَا كانَ لِلَّهِ أَنْ يَتَّخِذَ\nمِنْ وَلَدٍ\n، ودفعُ ما توهمه الرماة، فقد روى أنه- عليه الصلاة والسلام- قال لهم لما تركوا المركز: «ألمْ أعْهَد إليكُمْ ألا تَتْركُوا المركَزَ حتَّى يأتيكُمْ أمْري؟» قالوا: تَرَكْنا بقية إخْوانِنَا وُقوفاً، فقال النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «بل ظَننْتُم أنّا نَغُلّ ولا نقْسِمُ لكُمْ» . فنزلت الآية. وقيل إنه- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: بعث طلائع، فغنم رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم، وقسم على من معه فقط، فنزلت، فاسترجع ذلك منهم. وقيل: في قطيفة حمراء فُقدت يوم بدر، فقال المنافقون: لَعَلَّ رَسُولَ الله صلى الله عليه وسلم أَخَذَهَا، فنزلت.\nثم ذكر وعيد الغلول، فقال: وَمَنْ يَغْلُلْ يَأْتِ بِما غَلَّ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ أي: يأتي بالذي غله يحمله على رقبته، قال عليه الصلاة والسلام: «لا ألقى أحَدكُمْ يَوْمَ القيامة يجئ على رَقَبته بَعِيرٌ لَهُ رغاء، أو بقرة لها خُوَارٌ، أَوْ شَاةٌ تَيْعَرُ (١) .» ثم قال: «اللهُمَّ هَلْ بَلَّغْتُ؟ ثلاثاً» . كما في البخاري.\nثُمَّ تُوَفَّى كُلُّ نَفْسٍ جزاء ما كَسَبَتْ تاماً، وَهُمْ لا يُظْلَمُونَ بنقص ثواب مُطيعهم، ولا يزاد على عقاب عاصيهم وكان اللائق بما قبله أن يقول: ثم يوفى ما كسب. لكنه عمم الحكم ليكون كالبرهان على المقصود والمبالغة فيه، وأنه إذا كان كل كاسب مجزياً بعمله، فالغال مع عظم جرمه بذلك أولى. قاله البيضاوي.\nالإشارة: ما قيل في النبي- عليه الصلاة والسلام- يقال في ورثته الكرام، كالأولياء والعلماء الأتقياء، فإنهم ورثة الأنبياء، فيُظن بهم أحسن المذاهب، ويلتمس لهم أحسن المخارج، لأن الأولياء دلّوا على معرفة الله، والعلماء دلّوا على أحكام الله، وبذلك جاءت الرسل من عند الله، فلا يظن بهم نقص ولا خلل، ولا غلول ولا دخل، فلهم قسط ونصيب من حرمة الأنبياء، ولا سيما خواص الأولياء، ومن يظن بهم نقصاً أو خللاً، ويغل قلبه على شيء من ذلك، فسيرى وباله يوم تفضح السرائر، ثُمَّ تُوَفَّى كُلُّ نَفْسٍ مَّا كَسَبَتْ وَهُمْ لا يُظْلَمُونَ، فلحوم الأولياء والعلماء سموم قاتلة، وظن السوء بهم خيانة حاصلة. والله تعالى أعلم.\nفاعتقاد الكمال فى الأنبياء والأولياء مستوجب لرضى الله، والانتقاد عليهم موجب لمقت الله، كما أشار إلى ذلك الحق- جلت قدرته- فقال:","quran_ar":"وَما كانَ لِنَبِيٍّ أَنْ يَغُلَّ وَمَنْ يَغْلُلْ يَأْتِ بِما غَلَّ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ ثُمَّ تُوَفَّى كُلُّ نَفْسٍ مَّا كَسَبَتْ وَهُمْ لا يُظْلَمُونَ (161)","quran_en":"161. It is not for a prophet to misappropriate spoils. Whoever misappropriates shall bring what he misappropriated on the Day of Resurrection. Then every soul shall be paid in full what it earned, and none shall be wronged.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"Two vocalizations of yaghulla","html":"<p>I say: <strong>Ghulūl<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الغلول<\/bdi>) is secretly stealing from the war booty. Reading the verb with fatḥa on <strong>yāʾ<\/strong> and ḍamma on <strong>ghayn<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يَغُلَّ<\/bdi>) gives “It does not befit a prophet to take some booty secretly,” vindicating the Messenger. Reading ḍamma on <strong>yāʾ<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يُغَلَّ<\/bdi>) admits two interpretations: a prophet should not be betrayed by his community in the distribution of spoils—likewise commanders, although betrayal of a prophet is specially heinous; or a prophet should not be accused of treachery, just as “they do not call you a liar” can mean “they do not impute lying to you.”<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: Ghulūl ( الغلول ) is secretly stealing from the war booty. Reading the verb with fatḥa on yāʾ and ḍamma on ghayn ( يَغُلَّ ) gives “It does not befit a prophet to take some booty secretly,” vindicating the Messenger. Reading ḍamma on yāʾ ( يُغَلَّ ) admits two interpretations: a prophet should not be betrayed by his community in the distribution of spoils—likewise commanders, although betrayal of a prophet is specially heinous; or a prophet should not be accused of treachery, just as “they do not call you a liar” can mean “they do not impute lying to you.”"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Vindicating the Prophet and the warning against misappropriation","html":"<p>It cannot befit a prophet to take spoils secretly, for it is betrayal and incompatible with prophecy. The verse vindicates the Messenger—as in God’s saying, “It is not for God to take a child” [19:35]—and removes the archers’ suspicion. It is reported that, when they abandoned the station, he said to them: “Did I not charge you not to leave the station until my order came to you?” They answered: “We left the rest of our brothers standing there.” The Prophet replied: “Rather, you thought that we would misappropriate the spoils and allot you no share.” Then the verse was revealed. Another account says that he dispatched scouts, then the Messenger of God acquired spoils and divided them only among those with him. The verse was revealed, and he recovered those spoils from them. A third locates the occasion in a missing red cloak at Badr which hypocrites insinuated that the Prophet had taken. <span class=\"translator-note\"><strong>Translator’s note:<\/strong> Ibn ʿAjība cites these as alternate accounts, not a single harmonized event.<\/span><\/p><p>The verse then warns against ghulūl: whoever takes anything secretly will arrive carrying it on his neck on the Day of Resurrection. The Prophet said: “Let me not encounter any of you on the Day of Resurrection coming with a bellowing camel, a lowing cow or a bleating sheep upon his neck.” He then said, “O God, have I conveyed [the message]?” three times, as related in al-Bukhārī. Each soul then receives the full recompense for what it earned, no obedient person losing reward and no offender suffering more than is deserved. Whereas one might have expected “Then what he earned will be paid,” the verse generalizes to every soul, giving the argument greater force: if <i>every<\/i> earner is recompensed, how much more the one guilty of grave misappropriation. Ibn ʿAjība cites al-Bayḍāwī.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"It cannot befit a prophet to take spoils secretly, for it is betrayal and incompatible with prophecy. The verse vindicates the Messenger—as in God’s saying, “It is not for God to take a child” [19:35]—and removes the archers’ suspicion. It is reported that, when they abandoned the station, he said to them: “Did I not charge you not to leave the station until my order came to you?” They answered: “We left the rest of our brothers standing there.” The Prophet replied: “Rather, you thought that we would misappropriate the spoils and allot you no share.” Then the verse was revealed. Another account says that he dispatched scouts, then the Messenger of God acquired spoils and divided them only among those with him. The verse was revealed, and he recovered those spoils from them. A third locates the occasion in a missing red cloak at Badr which hypocrites insinuated that the Prophet had taken. Translator’s note: Ibn ʿAjība cites these as alternate accounts, not a single harmonized event. The verse then warns against ghulūl: whoever takes anything secretly will arrive carrying it on his neck on the Day of Resurrection. The Prophet said: “Let me not encounter any of you on the Day of Resurrection coming with a bellowing camel, a lowing cow or a bleating sheep upon his neck.” He then said, “O God, have I conveyed [the message]?” three times, as related in al-Bukhārī. Each soul then receives the full recompense for what it earned, no obedient person losing reward and no offender suffering more than is deserved. Whereas one might have expected “Then what he earned will be paid,” the verse generalizes to every soul, giving the argument greater force: if every earner is recompensed, how much more the one guilty of grave misappropriation. Ibn ʿAjība cites al-Bayḍāwī."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Presuming well of the inheritors of Prophets","html":"<p>What is said of the Prophet applies in an appropriate measure to his noble heirs—the friends of God (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الأولياء<\/bdi>) and scrupulous scholars (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">العلماء الأتقياء<\/bdi>). They inherit the work of prophets: the friends of God direct people to knowledge of Him, and scholars to His ordinances. One should think of them in the best ways and seek the best explanations of their conduct; one must not attribute imperfection, defect, misappropriation or corruption to them. They have a share in the reverence owed to the prophets, especially the elect among the friends of God. Whoever stores up suspicion against them will see its bitter consequence on the day secrets are exposed, for each soul is paid what it has earned. The “flesh” of the friends of God and scholars is deadly poison, and thinking ill of them is betrayal. God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"What is said of the Prophet applies in an appropriate measure to his noble heirs—the friends of God ( الأولياء ) and scrupulous scholars ( العلماء الأتقياء ). They inherit the work of prophets: the friends of God direct people to knowledge of Him, and scholars to His ordinances. One should think of them in the best ways and seek the best explanations of their conduct; one must not attribute imperfection, defect, misappropriation or corruption to them. They have a share in the reverence owed to the prophets, especially the elect among the friends of God. Whoever stores up suspicion against them will see its bitter consequence on the day secrets are exposed, for each soul is paid what it has earned. The “flesh” of the friends of God and scholars is deadly poison, and thinking ill of them is betrayal. God knows best."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Belief in the perfection of prophets and the friends of God brings God’s good pleasure, while finding fault with them brings His abhorrence.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Belief in the perfection of prophets and the friends of God brings God’s good pleasure, while finding fault with them brings His abhorrence."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C430.1","page":430,"ar":"تيعر: تصيح، واليعار: صوت الشاة.","en":"Tayʿaru means “it cries out”; yuʿār is the cry of a sheep.","anchor":"أَوْ شَاةٌ تَيْعَرُ"}],"english_characters":3317,"arabic_characters":2730},{"id":"q3-162-163","q":"3:162–163","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:162–163 · Divine pleasure and wrath, degrees of nearness and distance","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 430–431","cairo":"I:430–431","source_page_first":430,"source_page_last":431,"ar":"أَفَمَنِ اتَّبَعَ رِضْوانَ اللَّهِ كَمَنْ باءَ بِسَخَطٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ وَمَأْواهُ جَهَنَّمُ وَبِئْسَ الْمَصِيرُ (162) هُمْ دَرَجاتٌ عِنْدَ اللَّهِ وَاللَّهُ بَصِيرٌ بِما يَعْمَلُونَ (163)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: (أفمن اتبع رضوان الله) بأن اعتقد في نبيه الكمال، وأطاعه في وصف الجلال والجمال، وهم المؤمنون، حيث نزهوا نبيهم من النقائص، ومَن هَجَسَ في قلبه شيء بادر إلى التوبة، ثم اتصف بكمال الخصائص، هل يكون كَمَنْ باءَ بغضب مِنَ اللَّهِ؟ وهم المنافقون، حيث نافقوا الرسول واتهموه- عليه الصلاة والسلام- بالغلول.\nأو يقول: أَفَمَنِ اتَّبَعَ رِضْوانَ اللَّهِ بالطاعة والانقياد كَمَنْ باءَ بِسَخَطٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ بالمعاصي وسوء الاعتقاد وَمَأْواهُ جَهَنَّمُ وَبِئْسَ الْمَصِيرُ أي المنقلب، والفرق بين المصير والمرجع: أن المصير يجب أن يخالف الحالة الأولى، ولا كذلك المرجع. قاله البيضاوي.\nهُمْ دَرَجاتٌ عِنْدَ اللَّهِ أي: أهل الرضوان درجات متفاوتة عند الله، على قدر سعيهم في موجب الرضا، وأهل السخط درجات أيضاً، على قدر تفاوتهم في العصيان، وهو على حذف مضاف، أي: ذُوو درجات، وَاللَّهُ بَصِيرٌ بِما يَعْمَلُونَ فيجازي كلا على قدر سعيه.\nالإشارة: (أفمن اتبع رضوان الله) بتعظيم الأولياء والعلماء وأهل النسبة، كمن باء بسخط من الله بإهانة من أمر الله أن يُعظم ويُرفع، ومأواه حجاب الحس وعذاب البعد، وَبِئْسَ الْمَصِيرُ، فأهل القرب درجات على قدر تقربهم إلى ربهم، وأهل البعد درجات في البعد على قدر بعدهم من ربهم، بشؤم ذنبهم وسوء أدبهم، والله بصير بأعمالهم وما احتوت عليه قلوبهم.\nثم ذكر موجب التعظيم للرسول- عليه الصلاة والسلام- وهو كونه نعمة مهداة، فقال:","quran_ar":"أَفَمَنِ اتَّبَعَ رِضْوانَ اللَّهِ كَمَنْ باءَ بِسَخَطٍ مِنَ اللَّهِ وَمَأْواهُ جَهَنَّمُ وَبِئْسَ الْمَصِيرُ (162) هُمْ دَرَجاتٌ عِنْدَ اللَّهِ وَاللَّهُ بَصِيرٌ بِما يَعْمَلُونَ (163)","quran_en":"162. Is one who follows God's good pleasure like one who incurs wrath from God and whose refuge is Hell? What an evil destination! 163. They are degrees with God, and God sees what they do.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Riḍwān, sakhaṭ, maṣīr and marjiʿ","html":"<p>Is someone who seeks God's satisfaction by believing His Prophet to be perfect and obeying him in the manifestations of both Majesty and Beauty—namely the believers, who reject blameworthy imputations and, if any such thought occurs in their hearts, immediately repent and then become endowed with the perfection of special qualities—like one who comes burdened with His wrath? The latter are the hypocrites who impugned the Messenger and suspected ghulūl. Or the contrast is broader: obedient submission versus disobedience and corrupt belief. The latter's destination is Hell, an evil end. Al-Bayḍāwī observes that <strong>maṣīr<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مصير<\/bdi>) implies becoming different from one's former state, unlike <strong>marjiʿ<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">مرجع<\/bdi>), which simply means return.<\/p><p>“They are degrees with God”: those enjoying His pleasure have differing degrees according to their striving to please Him, and those incurring wrath also differ according to their acts of rebellion. A possessed noun is understood (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حذف مضاف<\/bdi>): <strong>dhawū darajāt<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ذوو درجات<\/bdi>, possessors of degrees). God sees their works and rewards or judges each accordingly.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Is someone who seeks God's satisfaction by believing His Prophet to be perfect and obeying him in the manifestations of both Majesty and Beauty—namely the believers, who reject blameworthy imputations and, if any such thought occurs in their hearts, immediately repent and then become endowed with the perfection of special qualities—like one who comes burdened with His wrath? The latter are the hypocrites who impugned the Messenger and suspected ghulūl. Or the contrast is broader: obedient submission versus disobedience and corrupt belief. The latter's destination is Hell, an evil end. Al-Bayḍāwī observes that maṣīr ( مصير ) implies becoming different from one's former state, unlike marjiʿ ( مرجع ), which simply means return. “They are degrees with God”: those enjoying His pleasure have differing degrees according to their striving to please Him, and those incurring wrath also differ according to their acts of rebellion. A possessed noun is understood ( حذف مضاف ): dhawū darajāt ( ذوو درجات , possessors of degrees). God sees their works and rewards or judges each accordingly."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Respect for the spiritual path and graded distance from God","html":"<p>Can one who seeks divine pleasure by revering the friends of God, scholars and people affiliated with the path be like one who incurs wrath by insulting those whom God has ordered to be honoured? Such a person's dwelling is the veil of sensory existence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">حجاب الحس<\/bdi>) and the anguish of distance, an evil end. People of nearness have degrees according to their approach to their Lord; people of distance likewise differ in their remoteness, as their faults and poor manners diverge. God knows their actions and what their hearts contain.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Can one who seeks divine pleasure by revering the friends of God, scholars and people affiliated with the path be like one who incurs wrath by insulting those whom God has ordered to be honoured? Such a person's dwelling is the veil of sensory existence ( حجاب الحس ) and the anguish of distance, an evil end. People of nearness have degrees according to their approach to their Lord; people of distance likewise differ in their remoteness, as their faults and poor manners diverge. God knows their actions and what their hearts contain."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>God next explains why the Messenger should be honoured: his coming is a divinely sent favour.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"God next explains why the Messenger should be honoured: his coming is a divinely sent favour."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1721,"arabic_characters":1426},{"id":"q3-164","q":"3:164","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:164 · The Messenger's three offices and the continuing masters of formation","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 431–432","cairo":"I:431–432","source_page_first":431,"source_page_last":432,"ar":"لَقَدْ مَنَّ اللَّهُ عَلَى الْمُؤْمِنِينَ إِذْ بَعَثَ فِيهِمْ رَسُولاً مِنْ أَنْفُسِهِمْ يَتْلُوا عَلَيْهِمْ آياتِهِ وَيُزَكِّيهِمْ وَيُعَلِّمُهُمُ الْكِتابَ وَالْحِكْمَةَ وَإِنْ كانُوا مِنْ قَبْلُ لَفِي ضَلالٍ مُبِينٍ (164)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: لَقَدْ مَنَّ اللَّهُ عَلَى الْمُؤْمِنِينَ حيث بَعَثَ فِيهِمْ رَسُولًا مِنْ أَنْفُسِهِمْ أي:\nمن جنسهم، أو من نسبهم، عربيّاً مثلهم ليفهموا كلامه بسهولة، ويفتخروا به على غيرهم. وتخصيص المؤمنين بالمنة، وإن كانت نعمته عامة لزيادة انتفاعهم على غيرهم لشرفهم وذكرهم به، حال كونه يَتْلُوا عَلَيْهِمْ آياتِهِ القرآن، بعد أن كانوا جاهلية لا يعرفون الوحي ولا سمعوا به، وَيُزَكِّيهِمْ أي: يطهرهم من دنس الذنوب ودرن العيوب، وَيُعَلِّمُهُمُ الْكِتابَ أي: القرآن، وَالْحِكْمَةَ أي: السنة، وَإِنْ كانُوا أي: وإنه، أي: الأمر والشأن كانوا مِنْ قَبْلُ بعثته لَفِي ضَلالٍ مُبِينٍ أي: ظاهر بيِّن.\nالإشارة: لقد منّ الله على المتوجهين إليه الطالبين لمعرفته، حيث بعث لهم من يأخذ بأيديهم، ويطوي مسافة البعد عنهم، وهم شيوخ التربية، يتلون عليهم آياته الدالة على كشف الحجاب وفتح الباب، ويزكيهم من دنس العيوب المانعة لعلم الغيوب، ثم يزكيهم من درن الحس إلى مشاهدة القرب والأنس، ويعلمهم الكتاب المشتمل على عين التحقيق، والحكمة المشتملة على التشريع وبيان الطريق، فيجمعون لهم ما بين الحقيقة والشريعة، وقد كانوا قبل ذلك في ضلال مبين عن الجمع بينهما. وهذه المنّة عامة في كل زمان، إذ لا تخلو الأرض من داع يدعو إلى الله، ومن اعتقد قطعه فقد قطع منّة الله، واستعجز قدرة الله، وسد باب الرحمة في وجه عباد الله، والعياذ بالله.\nولما استغرب الصحابة- رضى الله عنهم- ما وقع بهم يوم أحد، مع كونهم وعدوا النصر، نبههم الحق تعالى أن ذلك منهم بشؤم مخالفتهم، فقال:","quran_ar":"لَقَدْ مَنَّ اللَّهُ عَلَى الْمُؤْمِنِينَ إِذْ بَعَثَ فِيهِمْ رَسُولاً مِنْ أَنْفُسِهِمْ يَتْلُوا عَلَيْهِمْ آياتِهِ وَيُزَكِّيهِمْ وَيُعَلِّمُهُمُ الْكِتابَ وَالْحِكْمَةَ وَإِنْ كانُوا مِنْ قَبْلُ لَفِي ضَلالٍ مُبِينٍ (164)","quran_en":"164. God has bestowed a favour on the believers in raising up among them a messenger from their own selves, reciting to them His signs, purifying them and teaching them the Book and Wisdom, though previously they were in manifest error.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Recitation, purification and instruction","html":"<p>God bestowed a great favour upon believers by sending them a messenger <strong>from among themselves<\/strong>: one of their own kind, or of their own lineage, an Arab like them, so that they would readily understand his words and find distinction through him. Though his mission benefits all, the believers are singled out because they profit from it more fully and attain honour and renown through it. He recites God's signs to them—the Qurʾān—when they had lived in the Age of Ignorance without knowledge or experience of revelation. He purifies them (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">يُزَكِّيهِمْ<\/bdi>) of the contamination of sins and defects; teaches them the Book (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الكتاب<\/bdi>), the Qurʾān, and Wisdom (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحكمة<\/bdi>), the Sunna. Before his mission they were plainly astray. The construction “though they were ...” (<strong>wa-in kānū<\/strong>) can carry an understood matter or affair (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ضمير الشأن<\/bdi>).<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"God bestowed a great favour upon believers by sending them a messenger from among themselves : one of their own kind, or of their own lineage, an Arab like them, so that they would readily understand his words and find distinction through him. Though his mission benefits all, the believers are singled out because they profit from it more fully and attain honour and renown through it. He recites God's signs to them—the Qurʾān—when they had lived in the Age of Ignorance without knowledge or experience of revelation. He purifies them ( يُزَكِّيهِمْ ) of the contamination of sins and defects; teaches them the Book ( الكتاب ), the Qurʾān, and Wisdom ( الحكمة ), the Sunna. Before his mission they were plainly astray. The construction “though they were ...” ( wa-in kānū ) can carry an understood matter or affair ( ضمير الشأن )."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"The enduring favour of spiritual educators","html":"<p>Likewise God favours those turning to Him and seeking gnosis (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">معرفة<\/bdi>) by sending people who take them by the hand and fold away the distance separating them from Him: the spiritual educators (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">شيوخ التربية<\/bdi>). These recite signs showing how the veil can be removed and the door opened; purify seekers of faults that impede knowledge of the Unseen, then cleanse them of attachment to sensory limitation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">درن الحس<\/bdi>) until they witness intimacy and nearness; and teach the Book as the locus of realized truth (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">عين التحقيق<\/bdi>) and Wisdom as the locus of practical legislation (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">التشريع<\/bdi>) and clarification of the road. Thus they unite spiritual Reality (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحقيقة<\/bdi>) and Revealed Law (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشريعة<\/bdi>), while previously their disciples were plainly astray about bringing the two together. This favour continues in every age: earth never lacks an inviter to God. Whoever says this possibility has ceased denies God's favour, treats His power as impotent and closes the gate of divine mercy against His servants—God protect us.<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"Likewise God favours those turning to Him and seeking gnosis ( معرفة ) by sending people who take them by the hand and fold away the distance separating them from Him: the spiritual educators ( شيوخ التربية ). These recite signs showing how the veil can be removed and the door opened; purify seekers of faults that impede knowledge of the Unseen, then cleanse them of attachment to sensory limitation ( درن الحس ) until they witness intimacy and nearness; and teach the Book as the locus of realized truth ( عين التحقيق ) and Wisdom as the locus of practical legislation ( التشريع ) and clarification of the road. Thus they unite spiritual Reality ( الحقيقة ) and Revealed Law ( الشريعة ), while previously their disciples were plainly astray about bringing the two together. This favour continues in every age: earth never lacks an inviter to God. Whoever says this possibility has ceased denies God's favour, treats His power as impotent and closes the gate of divine mercy against His servants—God protect us."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>As the Companions wondered at Uḥud in view of the promised victory, God reminded them that the defeat arose through their own disobedience.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"As the Companions wondered at Uḥud in view of the promised victory, God reminded them that the defeat arose through their own disobedience."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1984,"arabic_characters":1557},{"id":"q3-165","q":"3:165","s":3,"surah":"Āl ʿImrān","title":"3:165 · The loss at Uḥud, self-accounting and the interplay of power and wisdom","source":"Cairo print · Volume I, pp. 432–433","cairo":"I:432–433","source_page_first":432,"source_page_last":433,"ar":"أَوَلَمَّا أَصابَتْكُمْ مُصِيبَةٌ قَدْ أَصَبْتُمْ مِثْلَيْها قُلْتُمْ أَنَّى هذا قُلْ هُوَ مِنْ عِنْدِ أَنْفُسِكُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدِيرٌ (165)\nقلت: الهمزة- للتفريع، و (لَمَّا) : ظرف، خافضة لشرطها، منصوبة بجوابها، وهي معطوفة على محذوف، أي: أكان ما كان يوم أحد، ولمّا أصابتكم مصيبة، قلتم ما قلتم، و (قد أصبتم) : جملة حالية.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: أحين أَصابَتْكُمْ مُصِيبَةٌ يوم أحد بقتل سبعين منكم، وقَدْ أَصَبْتُمْ مِثْلَيْها يوم بدر فقتلتم سبعين وأسرتم سبعين، قُلْتُمْ أَنَّى هذا أي: من أين أصابنا هذا البلاء وقد وعدنا النصر؟ قُلْ لهم: هُوَ مِنْ عِنْدِ أَنْفُسِكُمْ أي: مما اقترفته أنفسكم من مخالفة المركز، والنصر الموعود كان مشروطاً بالثبات والطاعة، فلما اختل الشرط اختل المشروط، إِنَّ اللَّهَ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدِيرٌ فيقدر على النصر بشرط وبغيره، لكن حكمته اقتضت وجود الأسباب والشروط لأن هذا العالم قائم بين قدرة وحكمة.\nأو: (قُلْ هُوَ مِنْ عِنْدِ أَنْفُسِكُمْ) باختياركم الفداء يوم بدر. رُوِيَ عن على رضي الله عنه قال: (جاء جِبْريل إلى النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم يوم بدر فقال: خيِّرْ أصْحَابَكَ في الأسارى، إن شاءوا القتل، وإن شاءوا الفداء، على أن يقتل مِنْهُمْ عاماً مقبلاً مِثْلُهمْ، قالوا: الفِدَاء ويُقْتَلُ مِنَّا.) . والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: إذا أصاب المريد شيء من المصائب والبلايا، فلا يستغرب وقوع ذلك به، ولا يتبرم منه، فإنه في دار المصائب والفجائع، «لا تستغرب وقوع الأكدار ما دُمتَ في هذه الدار، فإنما أبرزت ما هو مستحق وصفها وواجب نعتها» . وإذا كان أصابته مصيبة في وقت، فقد أصابته نعمٌ جمة في أوقات عديدة، فليشكر الله على ما أولاه، وليصبر على ما ابتلاه، ليكون صباراً شكوراً.\nقال الشيخ أبو الحسن- رضي الله عنه-: (العارف هو الذي عرف إساءاته في إحسان الله إليه، وعرف شدائد الزمان في الألطاف الجارية من الله عليه، فاذكروا آلاء الله لعلكم تفلحون) . وأيضاً: كل ما يصيب المؤمن فمن كسب يده، ويعفو عن كثير.\nوإن كان المريد وعد بالحفظ والنصر، فقد يكون ذلك بشروط خفيت عليه، فلم تتحقق فيه، فيخلف حفظه لينفذ قدر الله فيه، وَكانَ أَمْرُ اللَّهِ قَدَراً مَقْدُوراً.\nوليتميز الصادق من الكاذب والمخلص من المنافق، كما أشار إلى ذلك بقوله:","quran_ar":"أَوَلَمَّا أَصابَتْكُمْ مُصِيبَةٌ قَدْ أَصَبْتُمْ مِثْلَيْها قُلْتُمْ أَنَّى هذا قُلْ هُوَ مِنْ عِنْدِ أَنْفُسِكُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ عَلى كُلِّ شَيْءٍ قَدِيرٌ (165)","quran_en":"165. What! When a calamity befell you, though you had inflicted twice as much, did you say, “Whence is this?” Say, “It is from yourselves.” Truly God has power over all things.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"The interrogative hamza and lammā","html":"<p>I say: The interrogative <strong>hamza<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الهمزة<\/bdi>) marks an inferential continuation (<i>tafrīʿ<\/i>). <span class=\"translator-note\"><strong>Translator’s note:<\/strong> Cairo I:432 prints <bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">للتفريع<\/bdi>, “for inferential continuation,” retained here. Al-Bayḍāwī’s parallel exposition of 3:165 (II:47) instead has <i>taqrīʿ<\/i>, “rebuke,” together with <i>taqrīr<\/i>, “eliciting acknowledgement.”<\/span> <strong>lammā<\/strong> (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">لما<\/bdi>) is an adverb (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">ظرف<\/bdi>), its following condition governed by it in the genitive relation and the whole governed by its answer. The expression is connected with an omitted preceding phrase: “Did what happened at Uḥud happen, and when a calamity befell you did you say what you said?” “Though you had inflicted [twice as much]” (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قد أصبتم<\/bdi>) is a circumstantial sentence (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">جملة حالية<\/bdi>).<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"I say: The interrogative hamza ( الهمزة ) marks an inferential continuation ( tafrīʿ ). Translator’s note: Cairo I:432 prints للتفريع , “for inferential continuation,” retained here. Al-Bayḍāwī’s parallel exposition of 3:165 (II:47) instead has taqrīʿ , “rebuke,” together with taqrīr , “eliciting acknowledgement.” lammā ( لما ) is an adverb ( ظرف ), its following condition governed by it in the genitive relation and the whole governed by its answer. The expression is connected with an omitted preceding phrase: “Did what happened at Uḥud happen, and when a calamity befell you did you say what you said?” “Though you had inflicted [twice as much]” ( قد أصبتم ) is a circumstantial sentence ( جملة حالية )."},{"type":"exposition","title":"The conditions of the promise","html":"<p>When seventy of you were killed at Uḥud, though at Badr you had slain seventy enemies and taken seventy prisoners, you asked, “How could this calamity have overtaken us, when victory was promised?” Say: “It comes from yourselves,” from your disobedience in abandoning the appointed post. Victory was promised on the condition of steadfast obedience (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الثبات والطاعة<\/bdi>); when the condition (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الشرط<\/bdi>) failed, so did the conditioned outcome (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">المشروط<\/bdi>). God could give victory with or without such conditions, for He is powerful over all things, but His Wisdom (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحكمة<\/bdi>) willed there should be causes and conditions: this world subsists between divine Power (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">القدرة<\/bdi>) and Wisdom (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">الحكمة<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>Another explanation relates the loss to the Muslims' decision to accept ransom after Badr. ʿAlī is reported to have said that Gabriel offered them the option of killing the captives or ransoming them on condition that a corresponding number of their own be killed the following year; they chose the ransom and accepted the consequence. God knows best.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"When seventy of you were killed at Uḥud, though at Badr you had slain seventy enemies and taken seventy prisoners, you asked, “How could this calamity have overtaken us, when victory was promised?” Say: “It comes from yourselves,” from your disobedience in abandoning the appointed post. Victory was promised on the condition of steadfast obedience ( الثبات والطاعة ); when the condition ( الشرط ) failed, so did the conditioned outcome ( المشروط ). God could give victory with or without such conditions, for He is powerful over all things, but His Wisdom ( الحكمة ) willed there should be causes and conditions: this world subsists between divine Power ( القدرة ) and Wisdom ( الحكمة ). Another explanation relates the loss to the Muslims' decision to accept ransom after Badr. ʿAlī is reported to have said that Gabriel offered them the option of killing the captives or ransoming them on condition that a corresponding number of their own be killed the following year; they chose the ransom and accepted the consequence. God knows best."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"Every affliction considered against the gifts already received","html":"<p>When calamities befall a disciple he should neither find them strange nor resent them: this is an abode marked by affliction and grief. As the <i>Ḥikam<\/i> says: “Do not be surprised that sorrows occur so long as you remain in this abode: it has only brought forth what belongs to its description and is entailed by its nature.” If he is afflicted at one time, at many other times he has received blessings beyond reckoning. Let him thank God for the gifts and remain patient in the trial: then he is one who is both patient and grateful (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">صبّار شكور<\/bdi>).<\/p><p>Abū al-Ḥasan says: “The gnostic knows his offences amid God's kindness towards him, and knows the severity of time amid the gracious favours flowing to him. Remember God's benefits, that you may prosper.” Whatever misfortune befalls a believer comes by what his hands have earned, yet God pardons much. Nor should one conclude that a promise of preservation was absolute when it may have concealed conditions unknown to him. Those conditions may fail, allowing the divine decree to take effect: God's command is a destiny already decreed (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" dir=\"rtl\" lang=\"ar\">قدراً مقدوراً<\/bdi>).<\/p>","authorial":true,"text":"When calamities befall a disciple he should neither find them strange nor resent them: this is an abode marked by affliction and grief. As the Ḥikam says: “Do not be surprised that sorrows occur so long as you remain in this abode: it has only brought forth what belongs to its description and is entailed by its nature.” If he is afflicted at one time, at many other times he has received blessings beyond reckoning. Let him thank God for the gifts and remain patient in the trial: then he is one who is both patient and grateful ( صبّار شكور ). Abū al-Ḥasan says: “The gnostic knows his offences amid God's kindness towards him, and knows the severity of time amid the gracious favours flowing to him. Remember God's benefits, that you may prosper.” Whatever misfortune befalls a believer comes by what his hands have earned, yet God pardons much. Nor should one conclude that a promise of preservation was absolute when it may have concealed conditions unknown to him. Those conditions may fail, allowing the divine decree to take effect: God's command is a destiny already decreed ( قدراً مقدوراً )."},{"type":"transition","title":"","html":"<p>Thus the truthful are distinguished from the liar and the sincere from the hypocrite.<\/p>","authorial":false,"text":"Thus the truthful are distinguished from the liar and the sincere from the hypocrite."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2938,"arabic_characters":1949}];
